      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Helm in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "Helm is a package manager for Kubernetes. It helps teams define, package, configure, install, upgrade, and manage Kubernetes applications using reusable charts.\n\nInstead of maintaining many separate Kubernetes YAML files for every environment, engineers can use a Helm chart with templates and configurable values. Helm renders those templates into Kubernetes manifests.\n\nFor example, a team can package a web application with its Deployment, Service, and ConfigMap in one chart and install it into development, staging, and production using different values."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "Why is Helm used in DevOps?",
        "answer": "Helm is used in DevOps to simplify Kubernetes application deployment and configuration management. It provides reusable charts, release tracking, configurable values, and standard commands for installing and upgrading applications.\n\nIt reduces duplicated YAML and makes it easier to deploy the same application with different settings across environments.\n\nFor example, a CI/CD pipeline can use Helm to deploy an application with a development values file and later deploy the same chart with production-specific values."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is a Helm chart?",
        "answer": "A Helm chart is a collection of files that describes a Kubernetes application. It usually contains metadata, default values, templates, and optional supporting files.\n\nThe templates define Kubernetes resources, while values provide configurable inputs such as image tags, replica counts, service ports, and resource limits.\n\nFor example, a web application chart may contain templates for a Deployment and Service. The same chart can be reused with different values to deploy the application into multiple namespaces or environments."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is the Chart.yaml file?",
        "answer": "Chart.yaml is the metadata file located at the root of a Helm chart. It defines information such as the chart name, chart version, description, application version, and optional dependencies.\n\nHelm uses this metadata to identify and manage the chart. The chart version and application version represent different concepts: one identifies the chart package, while the other describes the application release represented by the chart.\n\nFor example, when a team changes a Deployment template, it may increment the chart version even if the application's software version remains unchanged."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is the values.yaml file in Helm?",
        "answer": "The values.yaml file contains default configuration values used by a Helm chart's templates. It allows chart authors to expose configurable settings without requiring users to edit the template files directly.\n\nValues may include container image details, replica counts, service types, ports, resource requests, and environment-specific configuration.\n\nFor example, a chart can define three replicas by default. A production values file can override that setting to five replicas without modifying the original Deployment template."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is a Helm template?",
        "answer": "A Helm template is a file that contains Kubernetes resource definitions combined with Go template expressions. Helm processes these expressions using chart values and other available template information to generate Kubernetes manifests.\n\nTemplates allow the same resource definition to produce different configurations depending on the supplied values.\n\nFor example, a Deployment template can use a value for the container image tag. Development and production deployments can therefore use different image versions while sharing the same template."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is a Helm release?",
        "answer": "A Helm release is an installed instance of a chart in a Kubernetes cluster. Each installation or upgrade creates or updates a release with a release name and associated revision history.\n\nA single chart can be installed multiple times using different release names or namespaces. Helm tracks the release information so that administrators can inspect, upgrade, or uninstall it.\n\nFor example, the same application chart can be installed as frontend-dev and frontend-prod with different configuration values."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is the difference between a Helm chart and a Helm release?",
        "answer": "A Helm chart is the reusable package that describes how an application should be deployed. A Helm release is a particular installed instance of that chart in a Kubernetes cluster.\n\nThe chart contains templates and default configuration. The release represents the deployed application and its release history.\n\nFor example, a company may maintain one nginx chart and install it several times as separate releases for testing, staging, and production."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is the helm install command?",
        "answer": "The helm install command installs a chart into a Kubernetes cluster and creates a new Helm release. The command requires a release name and a chart reference.\n\nAdministrators can supply configuration values, choose a namespace, and provide other installation options. The chart is rendered and the resulting Kubernetes resources are submitted to the cluster.\n\nFor example, helm install my-web ./web-chart installs a local chart as the release my-web. Engineers should verify the Kubernetes context and namespace before installing into production."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is the helm upgrade command?",
        "answer": "The helm upgrade command updates an existing Helm release using a chart and its configuration values. It is commonly used when a new application image, resource setting, or Kubernetes template change must be deployed.\n\nHelm compares the new rendered configuration with the existing release and applies the required Kubernetes changes.\n\nFor example, a pipeline can upgrade a release with a new container image tag after a successful application build. Teams should review the generated changes and monitor application health after the upgrade."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is the helm rollback command?",
        "answer": "The helm rollback command returns an existing Helm release to a previous revision recorded in its release history.\n\nIt is useful when a recent upgrade introduces problems such as an invalid configuration, broken Deployment, or incompatible application version.\n\nFor example, if release revision four causes application failures, an engineer may roll back to revision three after reviewing the incident. Rollback does not automatically reverse external side effects such as database migrations or data changes."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is the helm uninstall command?",
        "answer": "The helm uninstall command removes a Helm release and normally deletes the Kubernetes resources managed by that release.\n\nIt is commonly used to remove applications that are no longer required or to clean up temporary environments.\n\nFor example, a development team may uninstall a temporary test release after completing testing. Before uninstalling a production release, engineers must verify the release name, namespace, resource ownership, and any data persistence requirements."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "What is the purpose of helm list?",
        "answer": "The helm list command displays Helm releases available in the selected namespace or scope. It helps administrators identify installed releases and inspect their status and revision information.\n\nEngineers can use namespace-related options to inspect releases in a specific namespace or across namespaces.\n\nFor example, during deployment troubleshooting, an engineer can list releases to confirm whether the expected application release exists and whether Helm reports it as deployed, failed, or in another state."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is the purpose of helm status?",
        "answer": "The helm status command displays information about a particular Helm release. It can show the release state, revision, deployment notes, and resources or other details associated with the release.\n\nIt is useful when checking whether an installation or upgrade completed successfully.\n\nFor example, after a pipeline runs helm upgrade, an engineer can inspect the release status to determine whether Helm reports a successful operation. Kubernetes pod health should also be checked because a successful Helm operation does not guarantee that the application is functioning correctly."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is the purpose of helm history?",
        "answer": "The helm history command displays the revision history of a Helm release. It helps administrators identify previous deployment revisions and understand which upgrades or rollbacks occurred.\n\nEach successful release update can create a new revision. The history is useful when investigating a failed upgrade or deciding which earlier revision may be suitable for rollback.\n\nFor example, if an application became unhealthy after the latest deployment, an engineer can inspect Helm history to identify the previous revision and review the release timeline."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is the purpose of helm repo add?",
