      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "How is Helm used in a CI/CD pipeline?",
        "answer": "Helm is commonly used in CI/CD pipelines to validate charts, package deployment artifacts, and install or upgrade Kubernetes applications.\n\nA pipeline can run chart linting, render templates, validate generated manifests, and then deploy an approved chart version using environment-specific values.\n\nFor example, Jenkins can build an application image, publish it to a container registry, update the Helm image tag, and execute a controlled Helm upgrade. Teams should verify the Kubernetes context and monitor rollout health after deployment."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is the difference between Helm and kubectl?",
        "answer": "kubectl is the Kubernetes command-line tool used to inspect and manage Kubernetes resources directly. Helm is a package manager that organizes Kubernetes manifests into charts and tracks installed releases.\n\nEngineers use kubectl for operations such as inspecting pods, describing Deployments, and viewing events. They use Helm to install, upgrade, configure, and roll back packaged applications.\n\nFor example, Helm may deploy an application chart, while kubectl is used to inspect the resulting pods and troubleshoot why one of them is not starting."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Helm deployment failure?",
        "answer": "Start by checking the Helm release status and history to identify whether installation or upgrade failed. Review the command output and inspect the rendered manifests and values used for the deployment.\n\nNext, inspect Kubernetes resources using kubectl get, describe, and logs. Check events, pod scheduling, image pull errors, configuration references, resource limits, and readiness or liveness probes.\n\nAfter identifying the root cause, correct the chart or values and validate the rendered manifests before retrying. Avoid repeatedly upgrading production releases without understanding the original failure."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "tool": "Prometheus",
    "category": "Basic Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Prometheus?",
        "answer": "Prometheus is an open-source monitoring and alerting system widely used for monitoring applications, infrastructure, and Kubernetes environments.\n\nIt collects time-series metrics from configured targets, stores them with timestamps, and provides PromQL for querying and analyzing the data. It can also evaluate alerting rules and send notifications through Alertmanager.\n\nFor example, a DevOps team can monitor server CPU usage, memory consumption, application request rates, and Kubernetes pod health using Prometheus metrics."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "Why is Prometheus used in DevOps?",
        "answer": "Prometheus is used in DevOps to monitor system and application performance and detect operational problems before they cause major outages.\n\nIt collects metrics such as CPU usage, memory consumption, request counts, error rates, and response durations. Engineers can use queries and alerts to understand application behavior and investigate incidents.\n\nFor example, if an application starts returning too many HTTP 500 responses, Prometheus can collect the error metrics and trigger an alert so the operations team can investigate."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is a time-series database?",
        "answer": "A time-series database stores data points associated with timestamps. This structure is useful for monitoring because system and application metrics change continuously over time.\n\nPrometheus stores metric samples with timestamps and identifies each time series using a metric name and its labels.\n\nFor example, CPU usage can be collected every few seconds. Engineers can query the stored samples to understand whether CPU usage is increasing, decreasing, or remaining stable during an incident."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is a metric in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A metric is a numerical measurement that describes the behavior or condition of a system or application. Prometheus stores metrics as time-series data.\n\nExamples include CPU usage, memory consumption, HTTP request totals, request duration, and the number of active connections.\n\nFor example, an application can expose a metric representing the total number of HTTP requests. Prometheus collects that metric over time, allowing engineers to calculate request rates and identify changes in traffic."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is a Prometheus target?",
        "answer": "A target is an endpoint that Prometheus is configured to scrape for metrics. A target commonly exposes metrics through an HTTP endpoint, often at /metrics.\n\nPrometheus periodically connects to configured targets and collects the metrics they expose. Targets may include applications, exporters, and infrastructure components.\n\nFor example, a Node Exporter running on a Linux server exposes host metrics. Prometheus can scrape that endpoint to collect CPU, memory, filesystem, and network information."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is scraping in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "Scraping is the process through which Prometheus periodically requests metrics from configured targets and stores the returned samples.\n\nPrometheus uses a configured scrape interval to determine how frequently it collects metrics. Each scrape normally requests the target's metrics endpoint over HTTP.\n\nFor example, if a target is scraped every 30 seconds, Prometheus attempts to collect its metrics at that interval. Network failures or an unavailable endpoint can cause the scrape to fail."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is the Prometheus scrape interval?",
        "answer": "The scrape interval defines how frequently Prometheus collects metrics from a target. It can be configured globally or overridden for individual scrape jobs.\n\nA shorter interval provides more frequent observations but increases collection and storage overhead. A longer interval reduces overhead but may miss short-lived changes.\n\nFor example, infrastructure metrics may be collected every 30 seconds, while a highly sensitive application may require a shorter interval. The correct interval depends on monitoring needs and resource capacity."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is a Prometheus exporter?",
        "answer": "An exporter is a component that exposes metrics in a format Prometheus can scrape. Exporters are useful when the monitored system does not directly expose Prometheus-format metrics.\n\nFor example, Node Exporter exposes Linux host metrics such as CPU, memory, filesystem, and network statistics. Other exporters provide metrics for databases, network devices, and additional systems.\n\nPrometheus scrapes the exporter's HTTP endpoint and stores the returned metrics as time series."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is Node Exporter?",
        "answer": "Node Exporter is a Prometheus exporter used to expose hardware and operating-system metrics from Linux and other supported Unix-like systems.\n\nIt provides information about CPU activity, memory, filesystems, network interfaces, and other host-level resources through an HTTP metrics endpoint.\n\nFor example, a DevOps team can install Node Exporter on production Linux servers and configure Prometheus to scrape each server. This allows engineers to monitor host resource usage centrally."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is the Prometheus /metrics endpoint?",
        "answer": "The /metrics endpoint is an HTTP endpoint commonly exposed by Prometheus-instrumented applications and exporters. It provides metrics in the exposition format that Prometheus understands.\n\nPrometheus requests this endpoint during a scrape and stores the returned metric samples.\n\nFor example, an application may expose request totals, error counts, and request-duration metrics at /metrics. Engineers can inspect the endpoint to confirm whether metrics are being exposed correctly before investigating Prometheus collection."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is PromQL?",
        "answer": "PromQL stands for Prometheus Query Language. It is the query language used to select, calculate, and analyze time-series data stored in Prometheus.\n\nPromQL supports instant-vector and range-vector queries, label filtering, aggregation, mathematical operations, and functions for analyzing metrics over time.\n\nFor example, engineers can use rate() to calculate the per-second increase of a counter over a selected time range. This helps monitor application request rates and error trends."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is a Prometheus label?",
        "answer": "A label is a key-value pair attached to a Prometheus time series. Labels provide dimensions that allow metrics to be filtered and grouped.\n\nFor example, an HTTP request metric may have labels for method, status code, and application name. Engineers can use those labels to analyze traffic for a particular service or response code.\n\nLabels should be designed carefully because uncontrolled high-cardinality values, such as unique request IDs, can create too many time series and increase memory and storage usage."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "What is a metric name in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A metric name identifies the measurement represented by a Prometheus time series. It should describe the observed quantity clearly and follow Prometheus naming conventions.\n\nExamples include process_cpu_seconds_total and http_requests_total. A metric name combined with its label set identifies a particular time series.\n\nFor example, http_requests_total with different method and status labels can represent separate time series for different categories of application traffic."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What are the main Prometheus metric types?",
        "answer": "Prometheus commonly exposes four metric types: Counter, Gauge, Histogram, and Summary.\n\nA Counter increases over time and is commonly used for totals such as requests. A Gauge can increase or decrease and is useful for current values such as memory usage. A Histogram groups observations into configurable buckets, while a Summary calculates configured quantiles and observation counts or sums.\n\nFor example, an application may use a Counter for total requests, a Gauge for active connections, and a Histogram for request-duration measurements."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is a Counter metric in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A Counter is a cumulative metric that normally increases over time and can reset when the monitored process restarts.\n\nIt is useful for tracking events such as total HTTP requests, completed jobs, and application errors.\n\nFor example, an application can expose http_requests_total as a Counter. PromQL functions such as rate() or increase() can help calculate how quickly the counter grows over a selected time range."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is a Gauge metric in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A Gauge is a metric that represents a value that can increase or decrease. It is commonly used for measurements that describe the current state of a system.\n\nExamples include current memory usage, active connections, queue length, and temperature.\n\nFor example, an application can expose active_connections as a Gauge. Engineers can query this metric to understand the current number of active connections rather than the cumulative number of connections ever created."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "What is a Histogram metric in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A Histogram records observations and groups them into configured buckets. It also exposes the total observation count and sum.\n\nHistograms are commonly used to measure request duration, response size, and other distributions. They allow engineers to analyze how observations are distributed across defined bucket boundaries.\n\nFor example, an application can record HTTP request durations in buckets. PromQL can use histogram data to estimate latency percentiles across the observed requests."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is a Summary metric in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A Summary records observations and can expose configured quantiles, along with the total count and sum of observations.\n\nIt is commonly used for measuring distributions such as request latency. Unlike a Histogram, summary quantiles are calculated by the instrumented application and generally cannot be aggregated across multiple instances in the same way as histogram buckets.\n\nFor example, an application may expose request-duration quantiles for a particular process. Engineers should understand this limitation when designing metrics for distributed services."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What is a Prometheus scrape job?",
        "answer": "A scrape job is a configuration section that defines a group of targets and the settings Prometheus uses to collect metrics from them.\n\nA job can specify target addresses, scrape intervals, metrics paths, labels, and other scrape behavior.\n\nFor example, a job named node-exporter may contain the addresses of multiple Linux exporters. Prometheus uses that job configuration to discover and scrape the listed endpoints."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is the prometheus.yml file?",
        "answer": "The prometheus.yml file is the main configuration file used by Prometheus. It defines global settings, scrape configurations, rule files, and other monitoring behavior.\n\nThe scrape_configs section identifies which targets Prometheus should monitor. Rule files can define recording rules and alerting rules.\n\nFor example, an engineer can add a scrape job for Node Exporter and reload the Prometheus configuration using a supported reload method. Incorrect YAML or invalid configuration can prevent Prometheus from starting or reloading successfully."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is a Prometheus alerting rule?",
        "answer": "An alerting rule defines a PromQL expression that Prometheus evaluates periodically to identify conditions requiring attention.\n\nWhen the expression remains true according to the configured evaluation behavior, the alert can enter a firing state. Alerting rules can also include labels and annotations that describe the alert.\n\nFor example, a rule can detect when an application error rate exceeds an approved threshold. Prometheus evaluates the rule and sends firing alerts to Alertmanager."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is Alertmanager?",
        "answer": "Alertmanager is the component commonly used with Prometheus to manage alerts generated by Prometheus alerting rules.\n\nIt supports grouping, deduplication, routing, silencing, and inhibition of alerts. It can deliver notifications to configured receivers such as email, chat integrations, or incident-management systems.\n\nFor example, multiple alerts from the same application outage can be grouped into one notification. This reduces notification noise and helps operations teams focus on the incident."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is the difference between Prometheus and Alertmanager?",
        "answer": "Prometheus collects and stores metrics, evaluates PromQL queries, and determines when alerting rules should fire.\n\nAlertmanager receives alerts and manages their notification behavior. It groups related alerts, removes duplicates, applies routing rules, and sends notifications to configured receivers.\n\nFor example, Prometheus can detect that a server is unavailable, while Alertmanager decides which operations team should receive the notification and through which communication channel."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What is a recording rule in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A recording rule is a predefined PromQL expression whose result is periodically calculated and stored as a new time series.\n\nRecording rules help simplify frequently used queries and reduce the cost of repeatedly calculating complex expressions during dashboard or alert evaluation.\n\nFor example, a team can create a recording rule for an application's request rate. Dashboards can then use the stored result instead of recalculating the same complex query each time."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is the Prometheus expression browser?",
        "answer": "The Prometheus expression browser is the query interface provided by the Prometheus web interface. It allows engineers to enter PromQL expressions and inspect their results.\n\nIt is useful for checking whether metrics exist, testing label filters, and investigating monitoring data.\n\nFor example, an engineer can query an application's request counter and then test a rate expression to understand current traffic. This helps validate queries before adding them to dashboards or alerting rules."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the Prometheus TSDB?",
        "answer": "TSDB stands for Time Series Database. Prometheus uses its time-series storage engine to store metric samples and related metadata.\n\nThe storage engine organizes metric data into blocks and uses mechanisms such as the write-ahead log to support data ingestion and recovery.\n\nFor example, when Prometheus scrapes a server's CPU metric, the collected samples are stored in its time-series database so engineers can query historical resource usage."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is Prometheus data retention?",
        "answer": "Data retention defines how long Prometheus keeps stored metric data before removing older data according to its storage configuration.\n\nRetention settings affect disk usage, historical analysis, and operational requirements. Prometheus commonly supports time-based retention and storage-size limits.\n\nFor example, a monitoring server may retain local metrics for a defined number of days. Teams should size storage based on scrape volume, number of time series, retention requirements, and expected growth."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is service discovery in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "Service discovery allows Prometheus to find monitoring targets automatically from an external system or infrastructure source instead of relying only on manually maintained addresses.\n\nPrometheus supports several discovery mechanisms, including Kubernetes service discovery and cloud-related discovery integrations.\n\nFor example, in a Kubernetes environment, Prometheus can discover eligible pods or services based on configured discovery rules and labels. This helps monitoring adapt when workloads are created, removed, or rescheduled."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "How does Prometheus monitor Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "Prometheus monitors Kubernetes by collecting metrics from Kubernetes components, nodes, pods, and applications. It can use exporters, service discovery, and application instrumentation to gather the required information.\n\nTypical monitoring targets include kubelet metrics, node-level metrics, application metrics, and cluster component metrics where available.\n\nFor example, Prometheus can discover application pods through Kubernetes service discovery and scrape their metrics endpoints. Engineers can then query CPU, memory, request rates, and application errors."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is the difference between Prometheus and Grafana?",
        "answer": "Prometheus is primarily a metrics collection, storage, querying, and alert-rule evaluation system. Grafana is a visualization and observability platform used to build dashboards and explore data from supported sources.\n\nGrafana can connect to Prometheus and use PromQL queries to display metrics as graphs, tables, and other visualizations.\n\nFor example, Prometheus collects server CPU metrics, while Grafana displays those metrics in dashboards that help engineers monitor trends and investigate performance problems."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is the Prometheus web UI?",
        "answer": "The Prometheus web UI provides access to the Prometheus expression browser, target status, configuration information, and other operational pages.\n\nEngineers use it to execute PromQL queries, inspect scrape targets, and investigate whether Prometheus is collecting metrics successfully.\n\nFor example, if a dashboard shows missing data, an engineer can inspect the Targets page to check whether the corresponding exporter is UP or DOWN and then investigate the scrape error."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What does the UP metric mean in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "The up metric is a built-in metric generated for each configured scrape target. A value of 1 normally means the latest scrape succeeded, while a value of 0 indicates that the scrape failed.\n\nIt is useful for identifying unavailable exporters, unreachable endpoints, and scrape-related problems.\n\nFor example, if up is 0 for a Node Exporter target, engineers should check the target address, network connectivity, exporter process, firewall rules, and metrics endpoint."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is the rate() function in PromQL?",
        "answer": "The rate() function calculates the average per-second increase of a counter over a selected range of time-series samples. It is commonly used for monitoring request rates and error rates.\n\nCounters normally increase over time and may reset when a process restarts. rate() accounts for counter resets when estimating the average increase.\n\nFor example, rate(http_requests_total[5m]) calculates an average request rate over the last five minutes for the matching time series."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is the difference between rate() and increase() in PromQL?",
        "answer": "rate() calculates the average per-second increase of a counter over a selected range. It is commonly used when displaying request rates or event rates.\n\nincrease() estimates the total increase of a counter over the selected range. It is useful when engineers want to understand how many events occurred during a period.\n\nFor example, rate(http_requests_total[5m]) provides an average requests-per-second value, while increase(http_requests_total[1h]) estimates the number of requests during the last hour."
