      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is a PersistentVolume in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A PersistentVolume represents storage made available to a Kubernetes cluster, either provisioned in advance or dynamically through a storage provisioner. It abstracts storage details from the application Pod. For example, a database workload can use a persistent volume so its data survives Pod replacement."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What is a PersistentVolumeClaim?",
        "answer": "A PersistentVolumeClaim is a request for storage by a Kubernetes workload. It specifies requirements such as capacity and access modes, and Kubernetes binds it to a suitable PersistentVolume or provisions storage dynamically through a StorageClass. Pods reference the claim rather than directly depending on a particular storage device."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is a StorageClass in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A StorageClass describes a class of storage and the provisioner or parameters used for dynamic volume provisioning. It can define characteristics such as storage type, performance settings, and reclaim behavior depending on the provisioner. For example, a cloud cluster can use different StorageClasses for general-purpose and higher-performance block storage."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is the difference between ReadWriteOnce and ReadWriteMany access modes?",
        "answer": "ReadWriteOnce allows a volume to be mounted as read-write by a single node, though multiple Pods on that same node may be able to use it depending on the storage implementation. ReadWriteMany allows read-write mounting from multiple nodes when the storage backend supports it. Teams must select an access mode supported by the actual CSI driver and storage service."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is a StorageClass reclaim policy?",
        "answer": "The reclaim policy determines what happens to a PersistentVolume after its claim is released. Common policies include Retain and Delete, while supported provisioners may also offer other behaviors. Retain preserves the underlying storage for manual recovery or cleanup, whereas Delete can remove the provisioned storage according to the provisioner's implementation."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is a liveness probe in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A liveness probe checks whether a container is still functioning and should continue running. If the configured liveness probe fails enough times, Kubernetes restarts the container. It should detect unrecoverable application states rather than temporary dependency outages that the application can handle itself."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What is a readiness probe in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A readiness probe determines whether a container is ready to receive traffic. When readiness fails, the Pod is generally removed from the ready endpoints used by Services, without necessarily restarting the container. For example, an application can remain unready while it loads required configuration or establishes necessary local initialization."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is a startup probe in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A startup probe gives slow-starting applications additional time to initialize before liveness and readiness probes take over. While the startup probe has not succeeded, the other probes are held back according to Kubernetes probe behavior. It is useful for applications that perform lengthy initialization such as loading large models or warming caches."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the difference between liveness and readiness probes?",
        "answer": "Liveness determines whether a container should be restarted because it is unhealthy, while readiness determines whether it should receive service traffic. A service dependency being temporarily unavailable may make an application unready without requiring a restart. Correct probe design prevents unnecessary restarts and avoids routing requests to an application that cannot handle them."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "What is a rolling update in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A rolling update gradually replaces old application Pods with new Pods while attempting to maintain the availability specified by the Deployment strategy. Parameters such as maxSurge and maxUnavailable control how many additional or unavailable replicas are allowed during the rollout. Teams use rolling updates to release new versions without stopping the entire application at once."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "How do you roll back a Kubernetes Deployment?",
        "answer": "Use `kubectl rollout history deployment/name` to inspect available rollout revisions and `kubectl rollout undo deployment/name` to roll back to a previous revision when one is available. Then use `kubectl rollout status deployment/name` and inspect Pods and application health. A rollback restores a previous Pod template revision, but external database changes and other side effects may need separate recovery actions."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is a Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler?",
        "answer": "The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler automatically adjusts the replica count of a scalable workload based on observed metrics such as CPU or memory utilization, when supported metrics are available. It helps applications respond to changing demand without manually changing replica counts. Teams should configure resource requests and appropriate metrics because CPU utilization calculations depend on resource requests."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "What is a Vertical Pod Autoscaler?",
        "answer": "The Vertical Pod Autoscaler analyzes workload resource usage and can recommend or adjust CPU and memory requests and limits depending on its configuration and mode. It helps right-size workloads that have unsuitable resource allocations. Teams must understand its update behavior and possible Pod restarts before enabling automatic changes in production."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is a PodDisruptionBudget?",
        "answer": "A PodDisruptionBudget defines the minimum number or percentage of Pods that should remain available during supported voluntary disruptions. It helps protect application availability during operations such as node draining. It does not prevent involuntary failures or guarantee availability when the application has insufficient healthy replicas."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "What is node affinity in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "Node affinity allows a Pod to express scheduling preferences or requirements based on node labels. Required node affinity must be satisfied for scheduling, while preferred affinity influences scheduling without being mandatory. For example, a workload can require nodes with a specific hardware label or prefer nodes in a particular zone."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "What is pod affinity and pod anti-affinity?",
        "answer": "Pod affinity attracts Pods toward nodes or topology domains containing matching Pods, while pod anti-affinity discourages or prevents placement near matching Pods. These rules can help keep related workloads together or spread replicas across failure domains. For example, anti-affinity can reduce the risk of all replicas being placed on the same worker node."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What are taints and tolerations in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "A taint is applied to a node to repel Pods unless they have a matching toleration. A toleration allows a Pod to be considered for scheduling onto a tainted node but does not guarantee that it will be scheduled there. Teams use taints and tolerations to reserve specialized nodes for workloads such as GPU jobs or infrastructure services."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "What is a Kubernetes NetworkPolicy?",
        "answer": "A NetworkPolicy defines rules controlling network traffic to or from selected Pods, provided the cluster network plugin supports NetworkPolicy enforcement. Policies can restrict ingress, egress, or both using Pod selectors, namespace selectors, and IP blocks. For example, a database Pod can be configured to accept traffic only from Pods belonging to the application namespace and matching an application label."
      },
      {
        "number": 36,
        "question": "What is CoreDNS in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "CoreDNS is the cluster DNS service commonly used to resolve Kubernetes service names and configured DNS records. Pods use cluster DNS to discover Services without hardcoding their changing IP addresses. If service-name resolution fails, engineers should inspect CoreDNS Pods, logs, DNS configuration, and network connectivity."
      },
      {
        "number": 37,
        "question": "What is kube-proxy in Kubernetes?",
        "answer": "kube-proxy is a network component that implements Service networking behavior on nodes using supported packet-filtering or proxy mechanisms. It helps route traffic addressed to Kubernetes Service virtual IPs to appropriate backend endpoints. Some cluster networking implementations use alternative data planes, so the exact implementation depends on the cluster configuration."
      },
      {
        "number": 38,
        "question": "What is the Kubernetes scheduler?",
        "answer": "The Kubernetes scheduler selects a suitable node for newly created Pods that do not already have a node assignment. It evaluates resource requests, scheduling constraints, affinity rules, taints, tolerations, and other scheduling considerations. If a Pod remains Pending, engineers inspect its events and resource requirements to identify why no eligible node was selected."
      },
      {
        "number": 39,
        "question": "What is a Kubernetes admission controller?",
        "answer": "An admission controller intercepts API requests after authentication and authorization but before the object is persisted. Admission controllers can validate or mutate requests according to cluster policy. For example, a policy can reject Pods that request privileged containers or require approved image registries before workloads are admitted."
      },
      {
        "number": 40,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Pod stuck in Pending state?",
