      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins Pipeline is a defined sequence of stages and steps that automates software delivery activities such as checkout, build, test, security scanning, and deployment. It can be written using Declarative or Scripted Pipeline syntax in a Jenkinsfile. For example, a Java application pipeline can check out Git code, run Maven tests, build a JAR, create a Docker image, and deploy it to Kubernetes."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "What is the difference between Declarative and Scripted Pipeline?",
        "answer": "Declarative Pipeline uses a structured syntax with sections such as pipeline, agent, stages, and steps. Scripted Pipeline uses Groovy-based programming syntax and provides more flexibility for complex logic. Declarative is generally easier to maintain for standard CI/CD workflows, while Scripted can be useful for advanced dynamic pipeline behavior."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is a Jenkinsfile?",
        "answer": "A Jenkinsfile is a text file that defines a Jenkins Pipeline as code. It is usually stored in the application's source-code repository so pipeline changes can be reviewed and version-controlled alongside application changes. A Jenkinsfile can define agents, environment variables, stages, post-build actions, and deployment steps."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "What is the difference between an agent and a node in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "An agent is the execution environment where Jenkins runs pipeline steps, while a node represents a machine or configured execution resource connected to Jenkins. A controller can schedule work on agents using labels and availability. For example, a Linux agent can execute Maven builds while a Windows agent runs Windows-specific tests."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is an executor in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "An executor is a slot on a Jenkins node that can run one build or task at a time. A node with four executors can run up to four concurrent tasks, subject to resource availability and scheduling. Increasing executors without enough CPU, memory, or disk capacity can make builds slower and destabilize the agent."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins label?",
        "answer": "A label is a name assigned to one or more Jenkins nodes so jobs can select suitable execution environments. A pipeline can use an agent label such as linux, docker, or maven to request a matching agent. Labels help separate workloads and ensure builds run on machines with the required operating system and tools."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "What is the difference between Jenkins controller and agent?",
        "answer": "The Jenkins controller manages configuration, job scheduling, pipeline orchestration, and the user interface. Agents execute the actual build and test workloads assigned by the controller. Production environments generally avoid running heavy builds on the controller and instead use dedicated agents to improve isolation and scalability."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "How do you configure a Jenkins agent over SSH?",
        "answer": "An SSH agent is configured by providing the agent host, SSH credentials, remote working directory, and required connection settings. Jenkins connects to the machine and starts the agent process using the configured SSH launcher. Engineers should verify network reachability, host-key configuration, credentials, Java availability, filesystem permissions, and the agent log when connection fails."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins shared library?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins Shared Library provides reusable pipeline code that can be shared across multiple repositories and jobs. It commonly contains global variables, reusable steps, and supporting Groovy classes. For example, an organization can create a standard deployment function that handles image tagging, approvals, and Kubernetes rollout checks for many application pipelines."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "What is the vars directory in a Jenkins Shared Library?",
        "answer": "The vars directory contains globally accessible shared-library scripts that can be called by pipeline code. A file such as vars/buildApplication.groovy can expose a reusable step named buildApplication. This approach centralizes common pipeline logic and reduces duplicated code across teams."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What is the resources directory in a Jenkins Shared Library?",
        "answer": "The resources directory stores non-Groovy files used by a shared library, such as configuration templates, shell scripts, or other supporting resources. Library code can load these resources using supported library resource methods. It helps separate reusable pipeline logic from supporting files."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "What is the difference between parameters and environment variables in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Parameters provide user-selectable or externally supplied values when a job is started, while environment variables provide values available to pipeline steps during execution. Parameters are useful for selecting a deployment environment or release version, while environment variables expose configuration to commands. Sensitive values should be managed through Jenkins credentials rather than plain text parameters."
      },
      {
        "number": 13,
        "question": "How do you use credentials in a Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "Jenkins credentials store sensitive information such as passwords, tokens, SSH keys, and certificates. A pipeline can retrieve credentials using the credentials binding mechanism or supported credential-specific syntax. For example, a Git access token can be bound to a temporary environment variable for checkout, while masking and restricted permissions reduce accidental exposure."
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "question": "What is the Jenkins credentials binding plugin?",
        "answer": "The Credentials Binding plugin allows pipeline jobs to expose stored credentials to build steps in controlled ways, such as environment variables or temporary files. The withCredentials step limits the scope in which a credential is available. Teams should avoid printing secrets, passing them in command-line arguments, or storing them in workspace files."
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "question": "What is the difference between freestyle jobs and pipeline jobs?",
        "answer": "Freestyle jobs are configured mainly through Jenkins job settings and build steps in the user interface. Pipeline jobs define delivery workflows as code, usually in a Jenkinsfile, and support stages, conditions, parallel execution, and reusable logic. Pipelines are generally preferred for version-controlled and complex CI/CD workflows."
      },
      {
        "number": 16,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins multibranch pipeline?",
        "answer": "A multibranch pipeline automatically discovers branches in a source-code repository and creates pipeline jobs for branches containing a Jenkinsfile. It supports branch-specific builds and pull-request validation. For example, feature branches can run unit tests while the main branch can run additional release and deployment stages."
      },
      {
        "number": 17,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins webhook?",
        "answer": "A webhook allows a source-control platform to notify Jenkins when an event occurs, such as a push or pull request. Jenkins can use the notification to trigger a job without continuously polling the repository. Webhook troubleshooting includes checking the endpoint URL, network access, authentication, event configuration, and Jenkins trigger settings."
      },
      {
        "number": 18,
        "question": "What is the difference between polling SCM and using a webhook?",
        "answer": "SCM polling makes Jenkins periodically check the repository for changes, while a webhook sends an event notification when a configured repository event occurs. Webhooks usually reduce unnecessary repository checks and provide faster event-driven triggering. Polling may still be used when webhook delivery is unavailable or unsuitable."
      },
      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins post section?",
        "answer": "The post section in a Declarative Pipeline defines actions that run after pipeline execution or after particular conditions occur. Conditions include always, success, failure, unstable, and changed. For example, a pipeline can publish test reports, send notifications, or clean a workspace after the build finishes."
      },
      {
        "number": 20,
        "question": "What is the difference between archiveArtifacts and stash in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "archiveArtifacts stores selected build files as artifacts associated with a completed build so they can be downloaded later. stash temporarily saves files for transfer between stages or agents within the same pipeline run. Stash is intended for pipeline coordination, not long-term artifact management."
      },
      {
        "number": 21,
        "question": "What is the Jenkins stash and unstash mechanism?",
        "answer": "The stash step saves selected workspace files under a name, and unstash restores those files in another stage or agent during the same pipeline run. It is useful when a pipeline builds an artifact on one agent and tests or packages it on another. For large artifacts or long-term storage, an artifact repository is usually more appropriate."
      },
      {
        "number": 22,
        "question": "How do you run stages in parallel in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Declarative Pipeline supports parallel branches within a stage, allowing independent tasks to run concurrently. For example, Linux tests, Windows tests, and static code analysis can run at the same time when their dependencies permit it. Parallel execution can reduce pipeline duration but requires sufficient agent capacity and careful handling of shared resources."
      },
      {
        "number": 23,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins input step?",
        "answer": "The input step pauses pipeline execution and waits for an authorized user to approve or reject a requested action. It is commonly used for production deployment approval or release authorization. Teams should restrict who can approve the input and configure timeouts so a pipeline does not remain paused indefinitely."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What is the timeout option in Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "The timeout option limits how long a pipeline or a specific block can execute before Jenkins aborts it. It prevents jobs from waiting indefinitely for unavailable agents, stuck tests, or unresponsive deployment operations. For example, a deployment stage can have a defined timeout and report failure if the rollout does not complete."
      },
      {
        "number": 25,
        "question": "What is the retry step in Jenkins Pipeline?",
        "answer": "The retry step reruns a block of pipeline steps when an error occurs, up to the configured number of attempts. It can help with temporary network failures or transient external-service problems. It should not hide persistent application errors, and operations that are not safe to repeat should use appropriate idempotency controls."
      },
      {
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the difference between build and pipeline parameters?",
        "answer": "Build parameters are values supplied when a Jenkins job starts, while pipeline parameters are declared and consumed within a Pipeline definition. They can control choices such as branch, environment, release version, or deployment mode. Validation should be performed before using parameter values in shell commands or deployment operations."
      },
      {
        "number": 27,
        "question": "How do you trigger one Jenkins job from another?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins job can trigger another job using the build step in a Pipeline or configured upstream/downstream job relationships. Parameters can be passed to the downstream job when supported. For example, a successful build pipeline can trigger an integration-test job and then a deployment pipeline after the test result is verified."
      },
      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "What is an upstream and downstream job in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "An upstream job is a job whose execution can trigger another job, while the triggered job is called downstream. This relationship helps divide large delivery workflows into reusable stages. Teams should configure failure propagation and build-result handling carefully so a downstream failure is not incorrectly treated as a successful release."
      },
      {
        "number": 29,
        "question": "What is the Jenkins workspace?",
        "answer": "A Jenkins workspace is the directory where a job checks out source code and performs build operations on its assigned node. It can contain source files, temporary files, test reports, and generated artifacts. Workspace cleanup and correct file permissions help prevent stale files, disk exhaustion, and cross-build contamination."
      },
      {
        "number": 30,
        "question": "How do you clean a Jenkins workspace?",
        "answer": "The Workspace Cleanup plugin provides steps such as cleanWs for removing workspace files. A pipeline can clean before checkout or in a post section after execution. Before deleting files, engineers should ensure that required artifacts have already been archived or uploaded to an artifact repository."
      },
      {
        "number": 31,
        "question": "What is the Jenkins build result SUCCESS, FAILURE, and UNSTABLE?",
        "answer": "SUCCESS indicates the build completed successfully, FAILURE indicates that a build step or pipeline condition failed, and UNSTABLE indicates that the build completed but produced a result that does not meet all quality expectations. For example, a test report with failing tests may mark a build unstable depending on the configured reporting step."
      },
      {
        "number": 32,
        "question": "How do you publish JUnit test reports in Jenkins?",
        "answer": "The JUnit pipeline step reads XML test reports generated by test frameworks and displays test results in Jenkins. For example, a Maven project can generate reports under target/surefire-reports, which Jenkins can publish using the junit step. Publishing reports helps teams inspect failures, test trends, and flaky tests."
      },
      {
        "number": 33,
        "question": "How do you integrate SonarQube with Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Jenkins can invoke SonarQube analysis using the SonarScanner or a build-tool integration and communicate with a configured SonarQube server. The pipeline can wait for the quality gate result before continuing to later stages. Credentials, server configuration, scanner version, project key, and webhook connectivity should be checked when analysis or quality-gate reporting fails."
      },
      {
        "number": 34,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins quality gate?",
        "answer": "A quality gate is a set of quality conditions evaluated by a code-quality platform such as SonarQube. It can evaluate measures such as new-code coverage, bugs, vulnerabilities, and maintainability issues. A pipeline can stop promotion when the quality gate fails, preventing code that violates agreed quality standards from reaching later environments."
      },
      {
        "number": 35,
        "question": "How do you integrate Docker with Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Jenkins can build Docker images by executing Docker CLI commands or using supported container build tools and plugins. A typical pipeline checks out code, runs tests, builds an image, tags it with a version or commit identifier, scans it, and pushes it to a registry. The Jenkins agent needs the required build tooling and securely configured registry credentials."
      },
      {
        "number": 36,
        "question": "How do you integrate Kubernetes deployment with Jenkins?",
        "answer": "Jenkins can deploy to Kubernetes using kubectl, Helm, or a deployment tool from an appropriately configured agent. The pipeline authenticates using a restricted Kubernetes identity, applies manifests or a chart, and verifies rollout status. Production deployments should use namespace-scoped permissions, protected credentials, approval controls, and health validation."
      },
      {
        "number": 37,
        "question": "What is a Jenkins build discard policy?",
        "answer": "A build discard policy controls how many historical builds and artifacts Jenkins retains for a job. It helps control controller storage usage and keeps job history manageable. Teams should set retention according to audit, troubleshooting, and release requirements rather than deleting important release evidence too early."
      },
      {
        "number": 38,
        "question": "How do you manage Jenkins plugin updates?",
        "answer": "Plugin updates should be tested in a non-production Jenkins environment before being applied to a critical controller. Teams should review compatibility with the Jenkins core version and dependencies, take a backup, and schedule a maintenance window. Updating many plugins without testing can introduce dependency conflicts or break existing pipelines."
      },
      {
        "number": 39,
        "question": "How do you secure a Jenkins controller?",
        "answer": "Secure Jenkins by enabling authentication, configuring authorization with least privilege, using HTTPS, restricting administrative access, and protecting credentials. Keep Jenkins core and plugins updated, isolate build agents, and avoid executing untrusted pull-request code with privileged credentials. Backups and audit logging should also be part of the operational security process."
      },
      {
        "number": 40,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Jenkins pipeline that is stuck waiting for an agent?",
        "answer": "Inspect the queue item and pipeline console output to identify the requested agent label. Verify that an online agent has the matching label and an available executor. Check the agent connection status, Java compatibility, disk space, resource utilization, and controller-to-agent network connectivity. Correct the label or agent availability issue, then rerun the pipeline and verify that the expected stage begins executing."
      }
    ]
  },
