    "tool": "EKS",
    "category": "Intermediate Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "question": "What is Amazon EKS and how is it used in production DevOps environments?",
        "answer": "Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while customers manage worker capacity, workloads, networking choices, IAM integration, security policies, and application operations. EKS supports managed node groups, self-managed nodes, and Fargate profiles for different workload requirements. In production, teams deploy containerized applications through CI/CD, use IAM roles for access, integrate VPC networking, and monitor clusters through CloudWatch, Prometheus, and other observability systems. A healthy EKS control plane does not guarantee healthy worker nodes or applications. Production operations must validate API access, node readiness, CNI networking, DNS, storage, ingress, autoscaling, and workload availability independently."
      },
      {
        "number": 2,
        "question": "How do you connect to an EKS cluster using kubectl?",
        "answer": "The AWS CLI can update a kubeconfig file for an EKS cluster using aws eks update-kubeconfig --region REGION --name CLUSTER_NAME. This configures an authentication command that obtains credentials through AWS IAM. Verify the active context using kubectl config current-context and inspect access with kubectl get nodes. The caller needs appropriate AWS permissions to describe the cluster and Kubernetes authorization through the configured access entry or legacy aws-auth mapping, depending on cluster configuration. If connection fails, check AWS identity using aws sts get-caller-identity, region, cluster name, endpoint reachability, kubeconfig context, and IAM authorization. Never assume that AWS console access automatically grants Kubernetes API permissions."
      },
      {
        "number": 3,
        "question": "What is the difference between an EKS managed node group and a self-managed node group?",
        "answer": "An EKS managed node group lets AWS manage important parts of the EC2 worker-node lifecycle, including integration with EKS updates and managed scaling operations. A self-managed node group gives the customer more direct control over launch templates, Auto Scaling Groups, lifecycle hooks, and node configuration. Managed node groups simplify routine operations, while self-managed groups may be useful for specialized requirements. Both still require capacity planning, node security, Kubernetes version compatibility, and workload validation. During troubleshooting, inspect the node group's status, Auto Scaling Group health, EC2 instance state, launch template, bootstrap configuration, and node registration logs. Select the model based on operational requirements rather than assuming either option eliminates node maintenance."
      },
      {
        "number": 4,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS worker nodes that remain in NotReady status?",
        "answer": "Start with kubectl get nodes and kubectl describe node NODE_NAME. Inspect conditions such as Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure, and NetworkUnavailable, along with node events. Check EC2 instance health, kubelet service logs, container runtime status, disk capacity, security groups, route tables, and VPC CNI health. A node may be NotReady because it cannot reach the Kubernetes API endpoint, kubelet credentials are invalid, the CNI failed, or the operating system is under resource pressure. Inspect the aws-node and kube-proxy pods in kube-system. Resolve the actual node or network failure, then confirm the node becomes Ready and workloads can schedule successfully."
      },
      {
        "number": 5,
        "question": "What is the Amazon VPC CNI plugin and how do you troubleshoot pod networking issues in EKS?",
        "answer": "The Amazon VPC CNI plugin provides pod networking by assigning VPC IP addresses to Kubernetes pods and configuring the node network. It commonly runs through the aws-node DaemonSet in kube-system. If pods remain in ContainerCreating or networking errors appear, inspect kubectl get pods -n kube-system, kubectl logs for aws-node, node events, subnet free IP capacity, ENI limits, and security group rules. Check CNI configuration and relevant IP allocation metrics. Exhausted subnet addresses or instance ENI limits can prevent new pod IP allocation. Resolve subnet or capacity constraints through an approved network plan, then verify pod creation, DNS resolution, and connectivity to required services."
      },
      {
        "number": 6,
        "question": "How does IAM authentication and Kubernetes authorization work in Amazon EKS?",
        "answer": "EKS integrates AWS IAM identities with Kubernetes API authentication, but authentication and Kubernetes authorization are separate concerns. IAM credentials identify the caller, while EKS access entries and Kubernetes RBAC determine what the identity can do in the cluster. Older cluster configurations may use the aws-auth ConfigMap for identity mapping. If an AWS user can describe the cluster but kubectl returns Forbidden, check the AWS identity, EKS access configuration, group mapping, RoleBindings, and ClusterRoleBindings. Use kubectl auth can-i to test a specific operation. Grant the minimum required access and avoid assigning cluster-admin as a routine fix. Validate access with the intended CI or human identity."
      },
      {
        "number": 7,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS pods stuck in Pending status?",
        "answer": "A Pending pod may be unschedulable because of insufficient CPU or memory, node selectors, taints, tolerations, affinity rules, unavailable persistent volumes, topology constraints, or unavailable node capacity. Run kubectl describe pod POD -n NAMESPACE and inspect scheduling events such as Insufficient cpu, Insufficient memory, or untolerated taint. Check kubectl get nodes and kubectl describe nodes for allocatable resources and pressure conditions. Verify whether Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter is configured and whether it can provision the required instance type and subnet capacity. Correct resource requests or scheduling constraints only after understanding workload requirements. Confirm that the pod is scheduled, becomes Ready, and can serve traffic."
      },
      {
        "number": 8,
        "question": "What is the role of CoreDNS in EKS and how do you troubleshoot DNS resolution failures?",
        "answer": "CoreDNS provides cluster DNS resolution for Kubernetes Services and configured external names. If applications cannot resolve service names, inspect CoreDNS pods and deployment using kubectl get pods -n kube-system and kubectl logs -n kube-system deployment/coredns. Check the CoreDNS Service, endpoints, pod readiness, network connectivity, and configuration. Test DNS from a temporary diagnostic pod using nslookup or dig where available. Determine whether the failure affects all namespaces, only one node, or only external domains. Check node-local DNS configuration if deployed, VPC DNS settings, and upstream resolver reachability. Restore CoreDNS capacity or connectivity and verify both Kubernetes service discovery and required external DNS lookups."
      },
      {
        "number": 9,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot an EKS cluster API endpoint that is unreachable?",
        "answer": "First check the cluster state and endpoint configuration using aws eks describe-cluster --name CLUSTER_NAME --region REGION. Confirm whether the endpoint is public, private, or both, and inspect public access CIDRs and private endpoint requirements. For a private endpoint, the client needs network connectivity through the VPC, VPN, Direct Connect, or another approved path. Check DNS resolution, route tables, security groups, network ACLs, proxy settings, and the current kubeconfig context. Confirm AWS IAM identity and cluster authorization separately from network connectivity. Test from an approved host inside the required network path. Restore the correct network or access configuration and validate kubectl connectivity without broadening endpoint exposure unnecessarily."
      },
      {
        "number": 10,
        "question": "How do EKS managed node group updates and Kubernetes version upgrades work?",
        "answer": "EKS cluster upgrades and managed node group updates are separate operations that must be planned for Kubernetes version compatibility. The control plane is upgraded through EKS, while worker nodes must be updated to a compatible version and configuration. Before upgrading, review deprecated APIs, add-on compatibility, workload disruption budgets, capacity, and application compatibility. Update managed add-ons such as VPC CNI, CoreDNS, and kube-proxy according to the supported compatibility matrix. Roll nodes gradually, drain workloads safely, and monitor pod availability. Inspect EKS update status and node group health throughout the process. Validate node readiness, DNS, networking, storage, ingress, and application health before declaring the upgrade complete."
      },
      {
        "number": 11,
        "question": "What are EKS managed add-ons and how do you troubleshoot add-on failures?",
        "answer": "EKS managed add-ons provide AWS-managed lifecycle integration for cluster components such as VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, and other supported add-ons. If an add-on update or installation fails, inspect its status and health issues using aws eks describe-addon and review the associated Kubernetes resources and pod logs. Check version compatibility, IAM permissions, configuration conflicts, and resource availability. A managed add-on can fail because an existing custom configuration conflicts with the selected version. Back up relevant configuration and review the supported migration path before replacing settings. After remediation, verify add-on status, DaemonSet or Deployment readiness, and the cluster functionality that depends on the component."
      },
      {
        "number": 12,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS nodes that fail to join the cluster?",
        "answer": "When EC2 instances launch but do not register as Kubernetes nodes, inspect the node group status, instance system logs, bootstrap process, kubelet logs, IAM instance role, and cluster endpoint connectivity. Confirm that the node uses a supported AMI and bootstrap configuration for the cluster version. Check the node IAM role and required EKS worker-node permissions, cluster access configuration, security groups, DNS, and route tables. For private clusters, verify that the required AWS service endpoints and network paths are available. Review Auto Scaling Group health and launch template user data. Correct the failed bootstrap, IAM, or connectivity condition and verify that the node registers as Ready."
