        "question": "How do you safely drain an EKS worker node before maintenance?",
        "answer": "Before draining a node, identify the workloads running on it, available replacement capacity, PodDisruptionBudgets, local storage dependencies, and topology constraints. Cordon the node to prevent new scheduling with kubectl cordon NODE_NAME, then use kubectl drain NODE_NAME --ignore-daemonsets with carefully reviewed options. Do not automatically use --delete-emptydir-data or force deletion without understanding data-loss and availability implications. Monitor workload rescheduling and confirm that replacement pods become Ready. Check whether DaemonSets, static pods, or restrictive disruption budgets affect the operation. After maintenance, validate node health before uncordoning it. For production node group rotations, perform changes gradually and preserve sufficient capacity for application availability."
      },
      {
        "number": 24,
        "question": "What are PodDisruptionBudgets and how do they protect EKS workloads?",
        "answer": "A PodDisruptionBudget defines the permitted level of voluntary disruption for a group of pods, using either minAvailable or maxUnavailable. It helps protect application availability during operations such as node draining and managed node group maintenance. A PDB does not prevent every possible outage, and it does not protect against involuntary failures such as sudden node loss. If a node drain is blocked, inspect the PDB, selected pods, current ready replica count, and workload topology. A PDB requiring more available replicas than the application can provide may prevent maintenance indefinitely. Scale or repair the workload safely, review the availability requirement with the service owner, and retry the drain only after confirming that disruption is acceptable."
