      {
        "number": 28,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS nodes running out of pod IP addresses?",
        "answer": "Pod IP exhaustion can occur when a subnet has insufficient free addresses, a node reaches its ENI or IP capacity, or CNI warm-pool settings reserve addresses inefficiently for the workload pattern. Inspect aws-node logs and metrics, node conditions, pod creation events, subnet available address counts, and instance network limits. Review the VPC CNI configuration and supported prefix delegation settings for the chosen instance types and subnets. Confirm that the subnet has sufficient capacity before changing allocation behavior. Plan subnet expansion or additional subnet capacity through an approved network change when necessary. Validate by scheduling new pods across affected nodes and checking that pod IP allocation remains healthy."
