        "number": 37,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot EKS deployments that fail because of insufficient CPU or memory?",
        "answer": "Inspect pod scheduling events and node resource allocation to determine whether requests exceed available allocatable capacity. Use kubectl describe pod and kubectl describe node to review requested resources, limits, allocatable values, and current workload placement. Check whether pods are Pending due to insufficient resources or whether running containers are being OOMKilled because of memory pressure. Review actual usage over time using metrics rather than lowering requests blindly. Confirm that autoscaling can provision suitable instances and that node group limits, quotas, and subnet capacity allow expansion. Adjust workload requests and limits based on measured behavior, then verify stable scheduling and application performance."
