      {
        "number": 19,
        "question": "How do you investigate Maven dependency version conflicts?",
        "answer": "Dependency conflicts occur when different libraries request different versions of the same transitive dependency. Maven resolves conflicts using dependency mediation rules, which can result in a version different from what a developer expected. Run mvn dependency:tree and inspect the paths that introduce the conflicting artifacts. Use dependencyManagement to centralize versions or exclusions to remove an unwanted transitive dependency when compatibility has been verified. Check the selected version against the libraries that consume it and run relevant tests. Avoid adding arbitrary versions until the dependency graph is understood. In production, dependency convergence checks and automated vulnerability scanning can help detect inconsistent or risky dependency combinations before release."
