        "number": 2,
        "question": "What is the difference between hosted, proxy, and group repositories in Nexus?",
        "answer": "A hosted repository stores artifacts directly managed by the organization, such as internal application releases or snapshots. A proxy repository provides a local caching layer in front of an external repository, reducing repeated downloads and improving availability. A group repository combines multiple repositories behind one client-facing URL. For example, a Maven group may expose internal releases, snapshots, and a Maven Central proxy through one endpoint. In production, select the correct repository type based on whether the team is publishing internal content or consuming external dependencies. Check group membership and repository order when an artifact appears missing or an unexpected version is resolved."
