        "answer": "First establish the impact, affected application, environment, start time, recent deployments, and whether the issue affects all users or only a subset. Check load balancer and reverse-proxy health, DNS, network connectivity, Tomcat process status, listening ports, and application health endpoints. Inspect startup and application logs for deployment failures, exceptions, resource exhaustion, or dependency timeouts. Review JVM memory, garbage collection, thread pools, CPU, disk, and backend database or API health. Compare the current artifact and configuration with the last known-good release. Restore service using a safe rollback or targeted remediation, then validate external requests and critical business transactions. Preserve logs and thread or heap diagnostics where appropriate, identify the root cause, and add monitoring or deployment safeguards to prevent recurrence."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Tomcat virtual hosts are configured through Host elements in server.xml. Each Host represents a DNS hostname and can define its own application deployment base directory, aliases, and logging behavior. Configure the correct appBase and ensure the Tomcat service account has appropriate filesystem permissions. Add DNS and reverse proxy routing for the hostname and verify that the request reaches the intended Host. Test with explicit Host headers using curl before changing public DNS. Avoid overlapping host definitions and confirm that the default Host does not unintentionally serve an application for unknown hostnames.",
        "number": 21,
        "question": "How do you configure a Tomcat virtual host for multiple applications?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "The web.xml deployment descriptor defines application-level web configuration such as servlet declarations, servlet URL mappings, filters, listeners, welcome files, error pages, and security constraints. It can be packaged inside WEB-INF or supplemented by container configuration, depending on the setting. When an application behaves differently after deployment, inspect the effective descriptor, servlet mappings, filter order, and compatibility with the Servlet specification supported by the Tomcat release. Validate XML syntax and test application initialization in staging before promoting a descriptor change to production.",
        "number": 22,
        "question": "What is the purpose of Tomcat's web.xml deployment descriptor?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Welcome pages are commonly configured through the welcome-file-list element in the application's web.xml descriptor. Specify the intended landing resource, such as index.html or index.jsp, and ensure that the file exists in the deployed application at the expected location. Verify that security constraints and servlet mappings do not block the resource. Test requests to the application context root as well as direct requests to the welcome page. If the root URL returns 404, inspect the deployment contents, descriptor configuration, proxy rewrites, and application startup logs before changing server-wide defaults.",
        "number": 23,
        "question": "How do you configure a default welcome page for a Tomcat web application?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Application-specific error pages can be declared in web.xml using error-page mappings for HTTP status codes or Java exception types. Map each condition to a safe application resource that displays a useful message without exposing stack traces, filesystem paths, credentials, or internal infrastructure details. Confirm that the error page itself is accessible and does not cause recursive failures. Test representative 403, 404, and 500 responses in staging. Correlate user-facing error identifiers with server-side logs so support teams can investigate the underlying exception without exposing sensitive diagnostic information to clients.",
        "number": 24,
        "question": "How do you configure custom error pages for a Tomcat application?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Servlet filters are declared and mapped in the application's web.xml or registered through supported application initialization mechanisms. A filter can process requests before they reach a servlet and responses before they return to the client. Common uses include authentication, request correlation IDs, compression, security headers, and request validation. Filter ordering matters because one filter may depend on attributes established by another. When a filter causes unexpected behavior, inspect its mappings, dispatcher types, order, exceptions, and execution time. Test normal requests, forwards, includes, and error dispatches where applicable.",
        "number": 25,
        "question": "How do you configure request and response filters in a Tomcat web application?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "GlobalNamingResources defines resources that can be made available through Tomcat's global JNDI naming environment. A resource may represent a shared DataSource or another supported service, and an application can reference it through a ResourceLink in its context configuration. This can centralize configuration for multiple applications, but shared resources require careful ownership, credentials, connection limits, and lifecycle management. When a JNDI lookup fails, inspect the global resource definition, ResourceLink name, resource factory, driver availability, and application naming reference. Test resource access from the deployed application rather than relying only on XML validation.",
        "number": 26,
        "question": "What is the purpose of Tomcat's GlobalNamingResources configuration?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Tomcat access logging can be configured with an AccessLogValve associated with the relevant Host or Engine. Configure a clear log directory, filename pattern, timestamp format, and fields such as client address, request line, response status, response bytes, and processing time. Separate logs by virtual host when operational ownership or traffic analysis requires it. Ensure log directories are writable by the service account and protected from unauthorized access. Generate test requests to each hostname and verify that records appear in the expected files. Coordinate retention and rotation with the organization's logging platform.",
        "number": 27,
        "question": "How do you configure access logging separately for different Tomcat virtual hosts?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Tomcat and Java applications use temporary storage for generated files, uploads, compilation, and intermediate processing. A dedicated location can be configured through the supported JVM temporary-directory property or application-specific settings. Create the directory before startup, assign ownership to the Tomcat service account, and apply restrictive permissions appropriate to the workload. Confirm that the filesystem has sufficient capacity and monitoring. When temporary-file operations fail, inspect the effective JVM property, directory permissions, mount status, free space, and cleanup behavior. Do not place sensitive temporary files in publicly served application directories.",
        "number": 28,
        "question": "How do you configure Tomcat to use a dedicated temporary directory?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Identify every logging source, including Tomcat's JULI logs, application logs, access logs, and service-manager output. Configure an approved rotation mechanism with size or time-based policies, retention periods, compression, and secure permissions. Ensure that the rotation method is compatible with how each logging subsystem opens and writes files; some processes require a supported reopen or restart mechanism. Monitor disk utilization and alert before log files threaten application availability. Test rotation under controlled conditions, confirm new records continue to appear, and verify that retained logs remain available for incident investigation and compliance.",
        "number": 29,
        "question": "How do you manage Tomcat log retention and rotation in production?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Install a shared Tomcat distribution and create separate CATALINA_BASE directories for each instance. Give every instance its own server.xml, application deployments, logs, temporary directory, and service configuration. Assign non-conflicting shutdown, HTTP, and other connector ports, and run instances with clearly separated service identities or permissions where required. Define independent JVM memory settings based on total host capacity. Start each instance through its own service definition and verify its process arguments, listening ports, logs, and health endpoint. Document startup order, monitoring, and recovery procedures to avoid cross-instance operational mistakes.",
        "number": 30,
        "question": "How do you configure Tomcat to run multiple independent instances on one server?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "When TLS terminates at a reverse proxy, Tomcat may receive plain HTTP internally even though the client used HTTPS. Configure the proxy to send appropriate forwarded headers, such as the original scheme and host, and configure Tomcat's supported RemoteIpValve or equivalent mechanism to process headers only from trusted proxy addresses. Do not trust arbitrary client-supplied forwarding headers. Validate generated redirects, secure cookies, absolute URLs, and client IP logging. Test requests through the actual proxy path and verify that direct backend access cannot spoof trusted proxy metadata.",
        "number": 31,
        "question": "How do you configure Tomcat to trust requests forwarded by a reverse proxy?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Tomcat Connector configuration provides request-header size limits, with exact attribute names and defaults depending on the Tomcat release and connector implementation. Review the deployed version documentation before changing limits. If clients receive errors because headers are too large, inspect authentication cookies, proxy-added headers, and application behavior to identify the cause. Coordinate limits across the load balancer, reverse proxy, and Tomcat because the smallest limit may reject the request. Increase limits only to a justified value, consider memory implications under concurrency, and test oversized requests and normal traffic.",
        "number": 32,
        "question": "How do you configure maximum HTTP request header sizes in Tomcat?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Tomcat can serve static resources from the web application's document root through its default servlet. Ensure static files are packaged or deployed in the expected public resource locations and avoid exposing WEB-INF or other protected application directories. Configure caching and appropriate response headers through the application or supported container configuration. For high-volume static delivery, consider a CDN or dedicated web server rather than making Tomcat handle every asset request. Validate content types, cache behavior, compression, access restrictions, and deployment consistency across all Tomcat instances.",
        "number": 33,
        "question": "How do you configure a Tomcat application to serve static content efficiently?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Inspect the full ClassNotFoundException or NoClassDefFoundError stack trace and identify the missing class and the code path that requires it. Verify that the expected JAR is present in the application's WEB-INF/lib or an appropriate shared library location. Check dependency versions, packaging exclusions, classloader boundaries, and whether another library version is being loaded first. Compare the deployed artifact with the build output and inspect its contents using an archive listing tool. Avoid copying arbitrary JARs into Tomcat's global lib directory because that can create conflicts across applications. Rebuild and redeploy a dependency-consistent artifact.",
        "number": 34,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot a Tomcat application that cannot load a required Java class?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Tomcat uses classloaders to isolate container classes, shared libraries, and individual web applications. A web application commonly has access to its own WEB-INF/classes and WEB-INF/lib while container-level classes are loaded through parent classloaders. Delegation behavior affects which version of a class is selected when the same library exists in multiple locations. Classloader problems can cause NoSuchMethodError, ClassCastException, or unexpected dependency versions. Inspect the actual JAR locations and application classloading configuration, avoid duplicate libraries, and test shared-library changes against every application that depends on them.",
        "number": 35,
        "question": "What is Tomcat classloader delegation and why does it matter?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Session and application cookies should use security attributes appropriate to the application's authentication and cross-site requirements. Configure Secure so cookies are sent only over HTTPS, HttpOnly to restrict JavaScript access where applicable, and an appropriate SameSite policy to control cross-site sending. Set cookie path and domain deliberately to avoid exposing sessions to unrelated applications. The exact configuration mechanism can vary by Tomcat version and application framework. Inspect actual Set-Cookie response headers through the public endpoint and test login, logout, redirects, and legitimate cross-site workflows before production rollout.",
        "number": 36,
        "question": "How do you configure secure cookie attributes for a Tomcat-hosted application?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Timeouts exist at several layers, including the client, load balancer, reverse proxy, Tomcat connector, and application or database calls. Establish an intentional timeout hierarchy so upstream components do not abandon requests unexpectedly while backend work continues consuming resources. Identify whether the failure is a connection timeout, read timeout, idle timeout, or application execution delay. Correlate timestamps across proxy and Tomcat access logs and inspect slow application dependencies. Change only the relevant timeout after measuring normal and worst-case processing times, and verify behavior under load to prevent excessive request retention.",
        "number": 37,
        "question": "How do you configure request timeout behavior between Tomcat and an upstream proxy?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Java Management Extensions expose runtime management and monitoring information through MBeans. Tomcat and the JVM can expose attributes related to connectors, thread pools, memory, garbage collection, and other runtime components. Enable remote JMX only when required and secure it using network restrictions, authentication, and encryption appropriate to the environment. Prefer a private management network and avoid exposing management ports publicly. Connect with an approved monitoring client, inspect the relevant MBeans, and compare measurements with application and infrastructure metrics. Verify that monitoring access does not introduce unnecessary security exposure.",
        "number": 38,
        "question": "How do you use JMX to inspect a running Tomcat instance?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Graceful shutdown should allow the application to stop accepting new traffic and complete or safely terminate in-flight work according to the service's availability requirements. Coordinate load balancer draining with the Tomcat shutdown mechanism and application lifecycle callbacks. Ensure background workers, scheduled jobs, message consumers, and database operations release resources cleanly. Set an appropriate termination grace period in the service manager or orchestration platform. Test shutdown during normal traffic and verify that requests are not unexpectedly lost, resources are released, and the process exits within the expected time.",
        "number": 39,
        "question": "How do you configure graceful application shutdown behavior in Tomcat?"
