  {
    "tool": "Kubernetes",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "answer": "The Kubernetes control plane manages the desired state of the cluster. The API server exposes the Kubernetes API and validates requests, etcd stores cluster state, the scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes, and controller managers reconcile observed state with declared desired state. Kubelet on each node ensures assigned Pods run, while the container runtime creates containers. For production troubleshooting, inspect API server availability, etcd health and latency, scheduler events, controller logs, and node conditions. A healthy control plane does not guarantee application health, so validate the complete request path from API acceptance to workload readiness.",
        "number": 1,
        "question": "Explain the Kubernetes control plane architecture and how its components coordinate cluster operations."
      },
      {
        "answer": "The scheduler filters nodes that cannot satisfy Pod requirements and scores feasible nodes using scheduling policies and resource information. Constraints include CPU and memory requests, taints and tolerations, node selectors, affinity, topology spread, volumes, and scheduling policies. Investigate Pending Pods with kubectl describe pod and inspect Events for FailedScheduling messages. Compare requested resources with allocatable node capacity, inspect taints and affinity rules, and verify storage topology. Do not simply lower resource requests to force placement; confirm actual workload requirements and capacity before changing scheduling constraints or scaling the node pool.",
        "number": 2,
        "question": "How does Kubernetes scheduling work, and how would you investigate Pods remaining in Pending state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "etcd is the distributed key-value store that holds Kubernetes cluster state. Its availability depends on quorum, member health, network connectivity, disk performance, and consistent cluster configuration. Use supported snapshot procedures, protect snapshots with encryption and access controls, and store copies outside the cluster failure domain. Regularly test restoration in an isolated environment and document the exact Kubernetes and etcd recovery procedure. Monitor database size, backend commit latency, leader changes, and disk space. Never restore a snapshot casually into a live cluster because incorrect recovery can cause control-plane state inconsistency.",
        "number": 3,
        "question": "Explain Kubernetes etcd architecture, backup strategy, and recovery considerations for production clusters."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Start with namespace separation and combine it with RBAC, ResourceQuota, LimitRange, NetworkPolicy, Pod Security Standards, and admission controls. Use dedicated service accounts and restrict workload permissions according to tenant responsibilities. Apply default-deny network policies where supported and explicitly allow required communication. Prevent tenants from creating privileged workloads, hostPath mounts, or unauthorized cluster-scoped resources. Separate highly sensitive or mutually untrusted workloads into different clusters when namespace-level isolation is insufficient. Validate isolation using negative tests that attempt unauthorized API access, resource consumption, and network communication.",
        "number": 4,
        "question": "How would you design multi-tenant isolation in a shared Kubernetes cluster?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "NetworkPolicy controls ingress and egress traffic for selected Pods, but enforcement requires a network plugin that supports the resource. Policies are additive, and selecting a Pod for ingress or egress isolation changes the default behavior for that direction. Inspect policy selectors, namespace labels, Pod labels, allowed ports, protocols, and source or destination selectors. Verify that the installed CNI enforces policies and inspect its logs and configuration. Test DNS separately from application traffic and validate both directions of communication. Avoid assuming that creating a NetworkPolicy guarantees enforcement on every cluster.",
        "number": 5,
        "question": "Explain Kubernetes NetworkPolicy behavior and how you would troubleshoot blocked Pod communication."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Requests influence scheduling and represent the resource amount Kubernetes uses for placement decisions. Limits constrain resource consumption, with CPU throttling and memory OOM behavior having different effects. QoS classes are Guaranteed, Burstable, and BestEffort, based on container requests and limits. Under node pressure, kubelet can evict Pods according to resource pressure, priority, and usage relative to requests. Inspect node conditions, eviction events, cgroup metrics, and container termination reasons. Set requests from observed workload behavior and monitor throttling and memory pressure instead of applying identical limits to every workload.",
        "number": 6,
        "question": "How do Kubernetes requests, limits, QoS classes, and eviction thresholds interact under node resource pressure?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Deploy an ingress controller with sufficient replicas and distribute them across nodes and Availability Zones using topology constraints and appropriate disruption protection. Configure a reliable external load balancer, health checks, TLS certificates, DNS, and backend service routing. Define connection timeouts, request-size limits, rate controls, and safe defaults for exposed endpoints. Monitor controller availability, request latency, upstream errors, and certificate expiration. Test node failure, controller restarts, and backend unavailability. Remember that an Ingress resource defines routing intent; the installed controller and its configuration implement the actual traffic behavior.",
        "number": 7,
        "question": "How would you implement a highly available Kubernetes ingress architecture for production traffic?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "StatefulSets provide stable Pod identities, ordered deployment and termination behavior, and stable storage associations through volume claim templates. They are useful for applications that depend on predictable identities or persistent volumes, but they do not automatically provide database replication or application-level consistency. Review storage class topology, access modes, reclaim policies, update strategy, and readiness behavior. Use application-aware backup and recovery procedures, test replacement of individual replicas, and verify data integrity after rescheduling. For clustered databases, follow the database's own quorum, replication, and failover rules.",
        "number": 8,
        "question": "Explain Kubernetes StatefulSet behavior and how you would operate stateful workloads safely."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Admission control runs after API authentication and authorization and before an accepted object is persisted. Built-in admission plugins and external webhooks can validate or mutate resources to enforce organizational rules. Examples include rejecting privileged Pods, requiring approved image registries, enforcing labels, or injecting approved defaults. Configure webhook timeouts and failure policies carefully because an unavailable webhook can block API operations. Monitor webhook latency and errors, version policies through source control, and test both valid and invalid resource submissions before rollout. Use narrowly scoped rules to avoid disrupting critical system workloads.",
        "number": 9,
        "question": "How do Kubernetes admission controllers and admission webhooks enforce cluster governance?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Review supported version skew, deprecated APIs, CNI and CSI compatibility, admission webhooks, operators, and application dependencies. Back up cluster state using the supported platform procedure and test the target version in a representative environment. Upgrade control-plane components and node pools according to the managed or self-managed platform's documented sequence. Drain nodes with PodDisruptionBudgets and workload capacity in mind, then verify replacements become Ready. Check API deprecation warnings, scheduling, DNS, storage, ingress, and application SLOs. Maintain a tested recovery plan because Kubernetes downgrades are not always supported.",
        "number": 10,
        "question": "How would you perform a Kubernetes cluster upgrade while minimizing workload disruption?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A PodDisruptionBudget specifies the minimum available or maximum unavailable number of matching Pods during supported voluntary disruptions. It helps protect availability during operations such as node draining, but it does not prevent involuntary disruptions such as node failure. A restrictive budget can block maintenance if replicas are unhealthy or capacity is insufficient. Check selector correctness, replica count, current healthy Pods, and drain events before maintenance. Ensure workloads have enough replicas and spare capacity, and avoid setting budgets that make routine cluster operations impossible.",
        "number": 11,
        "question": "Explain Kubernetes PodDisruptionBudgets and their limitations during maintenance."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Inspect the Pod events, PersistentVolumeClaim, PersistentVolume, StorageClass, and CSI controller and node plugin logs. Determine whether the claim is unbound, the volume cannot attach, or the filesystem mount fails. Check access modes, zone topology, node compatibility, attachment limits, cloud permissions, and storage backend health. Verify that another node or Pod is not holding an exclusive attachment where the driver restricts it. Avoid deleting claims or volumes during investigation because this can destroy data. Correct the underlying storage or topology issue and verify application-level data integrity after recovery.",
        "number": 12,
        "question": "How do you troubleshoot Kubernetes persistent volume attachment and mount failures?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Kubernetes Services provide stable virtual endpoints for accessing selected Pods, while cluster DNS resolves Service names and configured external names. CoreDNS handles cluster DNS requests and depends on healthy Pods, service routing, network policies, and upstream resolver configuration. Diagnose failures by checking Pod resolv.conf, CoreDNS Pod readiness, logs, Service endpoints, DNS configuration, and network connectivity. Test short names, fully qualified service names, and external resolution separately. Inspect query latency and error rates. Avoid replacing service names with Pod IPs as a permanent workaround because Pod addresses can change.",
        "number": 13,
        "question": "Explain Kubernetes service discovery and how CoreDNS failures affect production applications."
      },
      {
        "answer": "A canary rollout introduces a new application version to a limited portion of traffic before increasing exposure. Deploy separate stable and canary workloads and use an ingress controller, service mesh, or delivery controller capable of controlled traffic splitting. Define automated evaluation criteria for error rate, latency, saturation, and business metrics. Increase traffic in measured stages and automatically pause or roll back when thresholds fail. Verify that database and message schema changes remain compatible with both versions during rollout. Record the release digest, traffic weights, evaluation results, and rollback decision.",
        "number": 14,
        "question": "How would you implement progressive delivery using Kubernetes canary deployments?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A service mesh adds a data plane, commonly proxies alongside workloads, and a control plane that distributes configuration for traffic management, identity, and telemetry. It can provide mutual TLS, retries, timeouts, traffic splitting, and service-to-service observability. It also adds resource overhead, configuration complexity, certificate lifecycle dependencies, and another failure domain. Monitor proxy health, control-plane synchronization, certificate expiry, and request behavior. Configure retries carefully to avoid retry amplification and cascading failures. Test sidecar injection, upgrades, and failure modes before enabling mesh features across critical workloads.",
        "number": 15,
        "question": "How do Kubernetes service meshes change traffic management and what operational risks do they introduce?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Use the cloud provider's workload identity integration or an equivalent federated identity mechanism so Pods receive short-lived, scoped credentials instead of static access keys. Bind a dedicated Kubernetes service account to the intended cloud identity and restrict its permissions to required resources and actions. Prevent unrelated Pods from using that identity and disable unnecessary automatic token mounting. Audit role assumptions and credential usage. Test token audience, trust conditions, and namespace boundaries. Rotate or revoke access through the identity provider rather than distributing permanent credentials in container images or manifests.",
        "number": 16,
        "question": "How would you secure Kubernetes workload identity when Pods need access to cloud services?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Inspect API server request duration, request rate, inflight requests, error codes, audit events, and control-plane resource utilization. Identify whether latency is caused by etcd operations, admission webhooks, expensive list or watch calls, authentication, or client retry storms. Review client-side QPS and burst settings and look for controllers repeatedly polling or requesting large object sets. Check etcd disk latency and API server logs. Optimize client behavior and webhook response time, and scale control-plane capacity where supported. Validate improvements using API latency percentiles and controller reconciliation health.",
        "number": 17,
        "question": "How do you investigate Kubernetes API server latency and request throttling?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "PriorityClass assigns relative priority values to Pods, allowing the scheduler to consider preemption when a higher-priority Pod cannot be scheduled. Preemption may evict lower-priority workloads, but it does not create additional physical capacity and can disrupt important services if priorities are poorly designed. Define a small, governed set of priority tiers and reserve critical values for platform components. Review PodDisruptionBudgets and preemption events, and test scheduling under realistic capacity pressure. Combine priority with resource requests, quotas, and capacity planning rather than using it as a substitute for sufficient infrastructure.",
        "number": 18,
        "question": "Explain Kubernetes priority classes and preemption, and how they should be used in production."
      },
      {
        "answer": "Collect infrastructure and workload metrics, centralized structured logs, distributed traces, Kubernetes events, and control-plane telemetry where available. Monitor node pressure, Pod restarts, scheduling delays, API server latency, DNS, ingress behavior, storage, and application service-level indicators. Use labels and metadata consistently while controlling high-cardinality dimensions. Define actionable alerts with ownership, severity, and runbook links. Ensure monitoring components have their own resource requests and availability design. Test alert delivery and dashboard usefulness during incidents, and retain enough historical data to identify gradual capacity and reliability trends.",
        "number": 19,
        "question": "How would you design Kubernetes observability for a large production cluster?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Define recovery objectives for cluster control state, application configuration, persistent data, secrets, images, and external dependencies. Maintain reproducible cluster infrastructure and platform configuration through version-controlled infrastructure as code and GitOps. Replicate application data using database or storage-specific mechanisms rather than assuming Kubernetes object backups protect application data. Store backups outside the primary failure domain and test restoration into a separate cluster. Document DNS or traffic failover, identity, network connectivity, and dependency recovery. Run timed exercises and verify application-level consistency before declaring recovery successful.",
        "number": 20,
        "question": "How would you design a Kubernetes platform for disaster recovery across regions or clusters?"
      }
    ]
