  {
    "tool": "Ansible",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "Q1. How does Ansible execution flow work internally from controller to managed nodes?",
        "answer": "Ansible starts on the control node by loading the inventory, configuration, playbook, variables, and applicable plugins. It evaluates plays and task order, resolves hosts and variables, and selects the required connection and execution plugins. For SSH-managed Linux hosts, it connects using the configured identity, transfers or executes the required module payload, gathers results, and reports changed, failed, skipped, or unreachable status. Handlers run when notified. In production, investigate controller logs, inventory resolution, SSH connectivity, Python availability, privilege escalation, and task output when execution differs from expectations."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. How would you design Ansible automation for thousands of production servers?",
        "answer": "Use a reliable inventory structure with environment, region, application, and role groups, and separate production credentials from lower environments. Tune forks and SSH settings according to controller capacity and target limits. Use serial batches, health checks, and max_fail_percentage to control rollout risk. Keep playbooks idempotent, use roles and tested collections, and store secrets in Ansible Vault or an approved secret manager. Execute through controlled CI/CD with approvals, logs, and clear rollback procedures. Test at small scale before increasing the rollout."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. What is the difference between linear and free strategies in Ansible?",
        "answer": "The linear strategy runs each task across the active hosts before moving to the next task, providing predictable task synchronization. The free strategy allows each host to proceed through tasks independently as soon as it is ready, which can improve throughput when hosts have different execution times. Free strategy can complicate coordinated operations and assumptions about all hosts reaching a task together. Choose based on application dependencies, rollout safety, and task behavior. Test concurrency and failure handling before using free strategy in production."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. How do you implement a zero-downtime application deployment using Ansible?",
        "answer": "Use a rolling deployment with serial batches and a load-balancer drain step before modifying each application node. Verify the node is removed from traffic, deploy the version, update configuration, restart or reload the service, and run local health checks. Re-register the node only after it is healthy, then continue to the next batch. Use max_fail_percentage and explicit failure handling to stop unsafe rollouts. Keep the previous artifact available for rollback and validate end-to-end service health after the full deployment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. How do you troubleshoot Ansible variable precedence problems?",
        "answer": "Start by identifying the variable name and every possible definition in role defaults, inventory group variables, host variables, play variables, task variables, extra vars, and registered values. Use ansible-inventory --host with the correct inventory to inspect resolved inventory variables, and run targeted tasks with verbosity when needed. Review include_vars, set_fact, role parameters, and extra-vars supplied by CI. Avoid defining the same variable in many layers. Use clear naming conventions and document intentional overrides to make production behavior predictable."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. How would you design Ansible roles for reuse across multiple applications?",
        "answer": "Separate responsibilities into focused roles such as operating-system baseline, users, monitoring agent, application runtime, and deployment. Define defaults in defaults/main.yml, required inputs in documentation, tasks in tasks/main.yml, templates and files in their respective directories, and handlers for service changes. Keep environment-specific values in inventory or external variable files rather than hardcoding them. Declare dependencies explicitly in meta/main.yml, pin collection versions, and test roles with representative operating systems. Reuse should not hide application-specific operational behavior."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. How do you use Ansible Vault securely in an enterprise CI/CD pipeline?",
        "answer": "Encrypt sensitive variable files or individual values with Ansible Vault and keep the vault password outside the repository. In CI/CD, retrieve the password from a protected secret manager or secured credential store and expose it only to the authorized job. Restrict pipeline permissions, prevent secret values from appearing in debug output, and avoid committing decrypted files or temporary artifacts. Rotate secrets when exposure is suspected. Validate access using a non-production pipeline and audit who can retrieve the vault credential."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. What is Ansible check mode and what are its limitations?",
        "answer": "Check mode, invoked with ansible-playbook --check, attempts to predict changes without applying them. It is useful for reviewing configuration and deployment impact before execution, but not every module fully supports check mode. Tasks that depend on earlier tasks creating files, registering actual runtime results, or executing custom commands may produce incomplete predictions. Review diff mode where supported and inspect module documentation. Never treat a successful check-mode run as proof that a production apply will succeed; validate in a representative environment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. How do you troubleshoot Ansible performance issues across a large inventory?",
        "answer": "Measure controller CPU, memory, network, task duration, and target response time before changing concurrency. Use callback timing or verbose output to identify slow tasks, excessive fact gathering, repeated package operations, and connection delays. Tune forks, SSH pipelining, persistent connections, and fact caching where appropriate. Reduce unnecessary gathered facts and avoid expensive shell commands when a dedicated module exists. Increase concurrency gradually because excessive forks can overload managed nodes, SSH services, or package repositories."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. How do you handle partial failures during an Ansible production rollout?",
        "answer": "Use serial batches and define an acceptable failure threshold with max_fail_percentage. Identify whether failures are unreachable hosts, task failures, application health failures, or dependency problems. Stop further rollout when the service safety threshold is exceeded. Preserve logs and failed-host details, then decide whether to retry, repair, or roll back the affected batch. Use block, rescue, and always sections for controlled recovery and cleanup, but do not hide the original failure. Verify the entire service fleet before resuming deployment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. How do you manage different operating systems with a single Ansible codebase?",
        "answer": "Use facts such as ansible_os_family, ansible_distribution, and ansible_distribution_major_version to select platform-specific tasks and package names. Organize operating-system differences into focused task files and templates while keeping common application behavior shared. Use conditionals only where platform behavior genuinely differs. Test supported distributions in CI or disposable virtual machines, and pin compatible collections. Avoid assuming that package names, service names, filesystem paths, or Python versions are identical across Linux distributions."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. What is the difference between delegate_to, local_action, and run_once?",
        "answer": "delegate_to runs a task on a different host than the current inventory host, such as calling a load-balancer API from a control node or management host. local_action is shorthand for executing a task locally on the controller. run_once executes a task once for the current batch rather than once per host, which matters when serial is configured. Understand variable context and delegation behavior before using them together. Validate that shared operations, such as database migrations, cannot execute multiple times unexpectedly."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. How do you build reliable Ansible handlers for service configuration changes?",
        "answer": "Handlers are notified by tasks and normally execute once at the end of the relevant play section, even when multiple tasks notify the same handler. Use them for actions such as restarting or reloading a service after a configuration template changes. Validate configuration before notifying a restart where possible, and use listen topics when several handlers share an event. Be careful with meta: flush_handlers because it changes execution timing. Confirm the service is active and healthy after the handler runs."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. How do you integrate Ansible with dynamic cloud inventory?",
        "answer": "Use the appropriate inventory plugin for the cloud provider and configure authentication through an approved identity mechanism. Define host filters and constructed groups using resource tags, environment, region, and application metadata. Validate discovery with ansible-inventory --graph and inspect resolved host variables with ansible-inventory --list. Ensure the CI identity has read-only discovery permissions and that inventory caching does not hide newly created or terminated hosts. Test empty-result behavior so a discovery failure cannot silently target zero production hosts."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. How do you make Ansible tasks idempotent when managing custom applications?",
        "answer": "Prefer purpose-built modules such as package, service, user, file, and template because they compare current state with the desired state. For custom applications, inspect configuration and version state before performing changes, and use changed_when and failed_when only when their conditions accurately represent the command result. Avoid unconditional shell commands that report changes on every run. Test by running the playbook twice: the first run should converge the system and the second should normally report zero changes."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. How do you troubleshoot Python interpreter problems on Ansible managed nodes?",
        "answer": "Inspect the failing task output and determine whether Python is missing, incompatible, or located at a different path. Check interpreter availability directly on the target and review ansible_python_interpreter settings at inventory or host level. Use raw for limited bootstrap operations when Python is not yet installed, then install a supported interpreter and rerun normal modules. Confirm the interpreter version is compatible with the installed Ansible and collection requirements. Avoid changing interpreter settings globally without validating all managed distributions."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. How do you use Ansible blocks, rescue, and always for production recovery?",
        "answer": "A block groups related tasks and can define shared conditions and privilege settings. If a task in the block fails, rescue can perform controlled recovery such as restoring a previous configuration or collecting diagnostic evidence. The always section runs cleanup or reporting tasks regardless of the block result. Rescue should not convert an unsafe deployment into an apparent success; preserve the original error and explicitly fail when recovery is incomplete. Test failure paths deliberately in staging before relying on them for production incidents."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. How do you control Ansible privilege escalation securely?",
        "answer": "Use become only for tasks that require elevated privileges and specify the required become method and user explicitly when necessary. Configure privilege escalation through controlled inventory or playbook settings and avoid storing passwords in plaintext. Use sudoers rules that grant only the commands and permissions required by automation. Separate connection identities from privileged execution identities, audit escalation activity, and test access with the actual CI service account. Do not solve permission failures by granting unrestricted root access to every automation user."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. How do you test and validate Ansible collections before production use?",
        "answer": "Review collection ownership, supported Ansible versions, dependencies, module behavior, and release history. Pin collection versions in requirements.yml and install them reproducibly in the automation environment. Run syntax checks, linting, module-specific tests, and integration tests against disposable targets. Verify privilege requirements, idempotency, check-mode behavior, and failure handling. Promote tested collection versions through controlled environments and retain the exact dependency versions used for each production deployment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. How would you design an enterprise Ansible automation architecture?",
        "answer": "Use a controlled automation execution environment with pinned Ansible and collection versions, managed credentials, and centralized logging. Organize inventories by environment and application ownership, store reusable roles in reviewed repositories, and keep secrets in Vault or an enterprise secret manager. Trigger playbooks through CI/CD or an automation controller with approvals, RBAC, and execution records. Apply rolling deployments, health checks, failure thresholds, and tested recovery procedures. Monitor job success, duration, unreachable hosts, and configuration drift across the fleet."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "tool": "Linux",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "Q1. How does the Linux boot process work from firmware to application services?",
        "answer": "The firmware initializes hardware and selects a boot device. The bootloader, commonly GRUB, loads the Linux kernel and initramfs into memory. The kernel initializes core subsystems, discovers hardware, mounts the initial root filesystem, and starts the configured init process, commonly systemd. Systemd then activates targets and dependencies, mounts filesystems, configures services, and starts application processes. When a server fails to boot, inspect console output, bootloader configuration, kernel messages, initramfs contents, filesystem health, and failed systemd units in sequence."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. How do you investigate high CPU utilization on a production Linux server?",
        "answer": "Start with uptime, top, or ps to identify load and CPU-consuming processes. Use mpstat to inspect per-CPU utilization and vmstat to distinguish runnable processes, I/O wait, and other system activity. Investigate process threads with top -H or pidstat, then inspect application logs and recent deployments. High load does not always mean CPU saturation; blocked I/O can also increase load average. Capture evidence before restarting services, and use profiling or application diagnostics to identify the actual workload causing the increase."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. How do you troubleshoot Linux memory exhaustion and the OOM killer?",
        "answer": "Inspect free -h, /proc/meminfo, vmstat, and process memory using ps or top. Review kernel messages with dmesg or journalctl -k for out-of-memory events and identify which process was killed. Distinguish application memory leaks, cache usage, memory limits, and insufficient capacity. Check container or cgroup limits when workloads are isolated. Do not assume that clearing filesystem caches fixes memory exhaustion. Capture process and service evidence, stabilize the workload, and correct memory sizing or application behavior."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. How do you investigate disk space exhaustion on Linux?",
        "answer": "Run df -h to identify full filesystems and df -i to detect inode exhaustion. Use du -xhd1 or targeted directory analysis to locate large usage without crossing filesystem boundaries. Check deleted-but-open files with lsof +L1 because their space remains allocated until the process closes the file. Inspect logs, temporary files, container layers, package caches, and application data. Before deleting anything, confirm retention requirements and ownership. Resolve the cause with log rotation, capacity planning, or application cleanup and verify available space afterward."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. What is the difference between a process and a thread in Linux?",
        "answer": "A process has its own virtual address space and operating-system resources, while threads within a process share the process address space and many resources but maintain separate execution contexts and stacks. Use ps -ef to inspect processes and ps -T or top -H to inspect threads. Thread-level CPU or memory behavior can reveal contention hidden at process level. When investigating application hangs, inspect thread counts, stack traces, file descriptors, and synchronization behavior rather than assuming every issue is a separate process."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. How do you troubleshoot a Linux service that fails to start under systemd?",
        "answer": "Check systemctl status <service> and journalctl -u <service> for the exact failure reason. Inspect the unit file, drop-in overrides, ExecStart path, user and group, environment variables, dependencies, and file permissions. Run the application command manually under the same execution identity when safe. Use systemd-analyze verify to validate unit syntax and daemon-reload after changing unit files. Correct the root cause, restart the service, and verify both systemd status and application-level health."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. How do you troubleshoot network connectivity from a Linux server?",
        "answer": "Check interface state and addresses with ip link and ip addr, then inspect routes using ip route. Test name resolution with getent hosts or dig and connectivity with ping, curl, nc, or traceroute as appropriate. Inspect listening sockets with ss -tulpn and firewall rules using the platform's firewall tooling. Compare source, destination, port, DNS result, route, and security policy. A successful ping does not prove an application port is reachable. Capture packet evidence with tcpdump when the failure remains unclear."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. How do you diagnose high disk I/O wait on a Linux server?",
        "answer": "Use vmstat, iostat -xz, and pidstat -d to identify device latency, queue depth, utilization, and processes generating I/O. Correlate the issue with application logs, database activity, backups, and storage changes. Inspect filesystem and block-device health and review cloud-volume performance limits where applicable. High I/O wait indicates CPUs are waiting on I/O and does not automatically identify a faulty disk. Reduce unnecessary I/O, tune the workload, or increase storage performance only after locating the bottleneck."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. How do Linux file permissions and ACLs affect application access?",
        "answer": "Traditional permissions define read, write, and execute rights for owner, group, and others, while ACLs allow additional user and group rules. Inspect ownership and modes with ls -l and inspect ACLs with getfacl. Check every parent directory because execute permission is required to traverse a directory. Also consider SELinux or AppArmor restrictions. Resolve access issues by granting the minimum required permissions and correcting ownership or ACL entries instead of applying broad chmod 777 permissions."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. How do you troubleshoot a Linux server that is reachable by IP but not by hostname?",
        "answer": "Check DNS resolution with getent hosts, dig, or nslookup and inspect /etc/resolv.conf and the system's resolver configuration. Verify search domains, DNS server reachability, records, TTL, and whether the application is using the expected resolver. Check /etc/hosts for conflicting entries and inspect split-horizon DNS behavior in cloud or corporate networks. Compare results from the affected host and a known-good host. Correct the record or resolver configuration and verify that cached responses have expired or been refreshed."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. What is the Linux load average and how should production teams interpret it?",
        "answer": "Load average represents the average number of tasks that are runnable or waiting in uninterruptible sleep over 1, 5, and 15 minute periods. Compare it with the number of CPU cores and inspect CPU utilization and I/O wait to understand the cause. A load average greater than the core count can indicate contention, but it is not a direct CPU percentage. A server with high load and high I/O wait may be storage-bound. Use vmstat, top, and iostat together before deciding to scale or tune workloads."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. How do you investigate a Linux kernel panic in production?",
        "answer": "Collect console output, crash dumps, kernel logs, and the exact kernel version before rebooting if evidence is still available. Inspect recent kernel updates, driver changes, hardware events, memory errors, and filesystem activity. Use configured kdump and crash tooling to analyze a vmcore when available. Compare the incident with known-good nodes and review changes around the failure timestamp. If recovery requires rebooting, use an approved procedure and preserve logs. Test a corrected kernel or driver in staging before broad rollout."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. How do you troubleshoot file descriptor exhaustion?",
        "answer": "Inspect process limits with ulimit -n and /proc/<pid>/limits, then count open descriptors through /proc/<pid>/fd or lsof -p. Check system-wide file handle usage in /proc/sys/fs/file-nr. Identify whether sockets, log files, pipes, or application handles are accumulating. Review application connection pooling, cleanup behavior, and long-lived sessions. Increasing limits may provide temporary relief but does not fix a descriptor leak. Apply appropriate service limits and validate descriptor counts under representative load."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. How do you troubleshoot time synchronization issues on Linux servers?",
        "answer": "Check system time and timezone using date and timedatectl, then inspect the active synchronization service such as chronyd or systemd-timesyncd. Review peer status with chronyc tracking and sources when using chrony, and inspect service logs for reachability or offset errors. Verify UDP port 123 access, DNS resolution, and approved time sources. Large clock differences can affect TLS, authentication, distributed logs, and databases. Correct the time source and verify synchronization and offset across related systems."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. How do you diagnose a Linux server that becomes unresponsive after deployment?",
        "answer": "Establish whether the host itself is unresponsive or only the application. Check console access, SSH, CPU, memory, disk, I/O wait, process state, and kernel logs. Compare the deployment timestamp with service restarts, configuration changes, package updates, and resource consumption. Inspect blocked processes and network sockets where access remains possible. Use a controlled rollback if the deployment is strongly correlated and service recovery requires it. Preserve logs and compare the failed host with a healthy peer before making permanent changes."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. How do you manage Linux kernel and package updates safely across production fleets?",
        "answer": "Maintain a tested package baseline and use controlled repositories with approved versions. Validate updates in non-production environments, review security advisories and compatibility, and schedule production rollout in batches. Check disk space, service dependencies, backups, and maintenance windows before updating. Use reboot coordination when kernel changes require it, and verify the running kernel with uname -r after reboot. Monitor service health and system logs during each batch, keeping a documented rollback or recovery plan."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. How do you investigate suspicious processes and possible compromise on Linux?",
        "answer": "Preserve evidence and follow the organization's incident response procedure before killing processes or deleting files. Inspect process ownership, command lines, parent processes, executable paths, network connections, scheduled jobs, authentication logs, and recent account changes. Use tools such as ps, ss, lsof, journalctl, and audit logs to establish activity. Isolate the host through approved network controls if compromise is suspected. Rotate exposed credentials from a trusted system and rebuild from a known-good image when integrity cannot be established."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. What is SELinux and how do you troubleshoot access denials?",
        "answer": "SELinux enforces mandatory access control using security contexts and policy rules beyond traditional Unix permissions. Check its mode with getenforce and inspect denial messages in audit logs using ausearch or journal logs. Review file contexts with ls -Z and compare them with expected service contexts. Restore standard contexts with restorecon when appropriate. Do not permanently disable SELinux as a routine fix. If policy changes are required, validate the minimum rule in a test environment and document its security impact."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. How do you troubleshoot Linux boot failures caused by filesystem or mount configuration?",
        "answer": "Use console or rescue access to inspect boot messages and identify the failed mount or filesystem. Review /etc/fstab for invalid UUIDs, device paths, filesystem types, and mount options. Compare configured UUIDs with blkid and inspect filesystem health using the correct filesystem-specific tools. A missing network or removable mount may block boot if configured as a required dependency. Back up configuration before editing, use systemd or rescue tools to validate mounts, and reboot only after the corrected configuration is verified."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. How would you establish a production Linux server hardening and operations baseline?",
        "answer": "Define a standard image with supported packages, security updates, minimal installed services, and controlled administrative access. Enforce SSH key or approved identity authentication, disable unnecessary remote access methods, configure host firewall rules, and apply SELinux or AppArmor policy. Centralize logs, time synchronization, monitoring, vulnerability scanning, and audit events. Set resource limits and reliable log rotation, and maintain tested backups. Track configuration drift and validate the baseline continuously through automation and periodic security reviews."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "tool": "ArgoCD",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "Q1. Explain how ArgoCD continuously reconciles Kubernetes desired state with live cluster state.",
        "answer": "ArgoCD compares Kubernetes manifests stored in Git with live resources in the destination cluster. It renders the desired manifests using Helm, Kustomize, plugins, or plain YAML, then calculates differences. Automated synchronization can apply changes without manual intervention, while self-heal can correct live drift. Production troubleshooting should examine the repository revision, rendering output, cluster credentials, resource health, application-controller logs, and sync operation history before attempting manual synchronization."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. How would you design a multi-cluster ArgoCD architecture for enterprise environments?",
        "answer": "Use a centrally managed ArgoCD control plane or separate instances where security boundaries require isolation. Register clusters using dedicated service accounts with minimum required permissions. Organize applications using AppProjects, repository allowlists, destination restrictions, and environment-specific configuration. ApplicationSets can generate applications for clusters selected by controlled labels. Monitor controller capacity, repo-server performance, cluster connectivity, and credential expiration. Test cluster onboarding, credential rotation, and recovery before adopting the architecture for production workloads."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. Explain automated sync, self-heal, and prune in ArgoCD.",
        "answer": "Automated sync applies desired Git changes without requiring a manual sync request. Self-heal reconciles live changes that differ from Git, while prune deletes managed resources that no longer appear in the desired manifests. These controls have different operational risks: self-heal may reverse an emergency manual correction, and prune may delete resources unexpectedly. Enable them selectively, understand resource ownership, and protect persistent or independently managed resources with appropriate policies and review procedures."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. How do you troubleshoot an ArgoCD application that remains OutOfSync after synchronization?",
        "answer": "Inspect the application diff and resource tree to identify the exact fields that remain different. Confirm the expected Git revision and verify that the source renders successfully. Investigate admission webhooks, defaulted fields, mutating controllers, and operators that may continuously modify resources. Review ignoreDifferences rules because broad exclusions can conceal real drift. Examine application-controller logs and compare live YAML with rendered desired manifests. Correct the source of the difference and confirm the application reaches Synced without hiding important configuration changes."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. How do AppProjects improve ArgoCD security?",
        "answer": "AppProjects restrict the Git repositories, destination clusters, namespaces, and resource kinds that applications may use. They can also define project-level roles and permissions. Create separate projects around application ownership and environment boundaries rather than allowing every application to use the unrestricted default project. Deny unnecessary cluster-scoped resource creation, maintain repository allowlists, and review destination changes as security-sensitive modifications. Test that an application cannot deploy outside its authorized cluster or namespace."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. Explain the ArgoCD ApplicationSet controller and its production use cases.",
        "answer": "ApplicationSet generates multiple ArgoCD Application resources from templates and generator inputs. Cluster, list, Git directory, Git file, matrix, and merge generators support different discovery and combination patterns. A cluster generator can create an application for every registered cluster matching environment labels. This reduces repetitive definitions but can create excessive applications if selectors are too broad. Validate generated resources, protect cluster labels, review template changes, and monitor application counts and controller load."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. How do you manage secrets securely in an ArgoCD GitOps workflow?",
        "answer": "Never commit plaintext credentials, tokens, or private keys to Git. Use an approved external secrets operator, secret-management integration, or encrypted manifests with controlled decryption. Restrict repository and plugin access, protect decryption keys, and ensure secret values do not appear in logs or rendered diffs. Rotate credentials and audit access. Kubernetes Secret data is base64 encoded by default and should not be considered confidential without appropriate encryption and RBAC controls. Validate the entire flow from Git retrieval to cluster application."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. How do you troubleshoot an ArgoCD repository connection failure?",
        "answer": "Inspect repository connection status and repo-server logs for authentication, DNS, TLS, timeout, and permission errors. Verify the repository URL, deploy key, token scope, certificate chain, and repository permissions. Test DNS resolution and network connectivity from the repo-server environment. Check whether a credential expired or repository access changed. Update credentials through the approved secret mechanism, refresh the application, and confirm that ArgoCD can retrieve the expected revision."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. What is the role of argocd-repo-server and how do you scale it?",
        "answer": "The repo-server retrieves repository content and generates manifests using tools such as Helm, Kustomize, and config-management plugins. It is critical to application comparison and synchronization. Investigate CPU, memory, request latency, repository access, rendering errors, and plugin execution time when applications remain Unknown. Scale replicas according to workload, configure resource requests and limits, and optimize repository size and caching. Ensure custom tools and plugins are consistently installed and configured across replicas."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. How would you implement progressive delivery using ArgoCD?",
        "answer": "Use ArgoCD to manage desired configuration for a progressive delivery controller such as Argo Rollouts. Define canary or blue-green steps, traffic shifting, analysis templates, metric checks, and promotion or rollback criteria in Git. The rollout controller manages staged delivery while ArgoCD maintains the declared resources. Integrate monitoring metrics and define clear failure thresholds. Test rollback behavior under realistic traffic and ensure health assessments reflect actual application availability rather than only Kubernetes resource creation."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. How do you troubleshoot an ArgoCD application stuck in Progressing status?",
        "answer": "Inspect the resource tree to identify which resource has not reached its expected health state. Check Kubernetes events, pod conditions, deployment rollout status, readiness probes, image pulls, PVC binding, and custom health checks. Review application-controller logs for health assessment errors. A pod can be Running while failing readiness, and a custom resource may need a health customization. Resolve the underlying workload problem and verify that the ArgoCD application eventually reports Healthy."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. What are sync waves and hooks in ArgoCD?",
        "answer": "Sync waves order resources during synchronization using annotations that assign resources to different wave numbers. They help deploy dependencies in a controlled sequence, such as namespaces before workloads or configuration before applications. Resource hooks support lifecycle actions such as pre-sync validation, sync-time jobs, and post-sync verification. Hooks need clear deletion policies and timeout behavior. Avoid relying on ordering alone for application readiness; validate dependencies and health conditions explicitly before promoting a production deployment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. How do you troubleshoot ArgoCD sync failures caused by immutable Kubernetes fields?",
        "answer": "Inspect the sync operation error and identify the resource field Kubernetes refuses to modify. Compare the rendered manifest with the live object and determine whether the field is immutable for that resource type. Decide whether a controlled replacement is safe, whether data migration is needed, or whether the manifest should preserve the existing value. Do not force deletion of stateful resources without a recovery plan. Test replacement behavior in staging and verify dependent services before production synchronization."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. How do you manage ArgoCD application dependencies across multiple repositories?",
        "answer": "Keep application dependencies explicit and avoid assuming that separate ArgoCD applications synchronize in a guaranteed global order. Use app-of-apps patterns, ApplicationSets, sync waves where applicable, and health checks to organize deployment stages. For critical dependencies such as databases and shared platform services, define readiness gates and independent change approvals. Avoid circular dependencies between applications. Test installation, upgrade, and rollback sequences from a clean environment so deployment order is reproducible."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. How do you troubleshoot ArgoCD application-controller performance problems?",
        "answer": "Measure controller CPU, memory, reconciliation duration, queue depth, and Kubernetes API request latency. Identify whether a large number of applications, frequent Git changes, excessive resource tracking, or expensive health customizations are creating load. Inspect controller logs and metrics, then review resource requests, concurrency settings, and application boundaries. Scale only after identifying the bottleneck and checking API-server capacity. Validate reconciliation latency and cluster API load after each tuning change."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. How do you safely handle emergency production changes in a GitOps-managed cluster?",
        "answer": "Follow the organization's incident process and record why an emergency change is required. If a live modification is necessary, understand whether self-heal will immediately reverse it. Make the corresponding Git change as soon as possible so the declared desired state matches the approved live correction. Review the diff, validate the resource, and ensure the change is represented in the normal deployment history. Avoid disabling reconciliation broadly or leaving undocumented manual changes in production."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. How do you configure custom health checks for ArgoCD custom resources?",
        "answer": "When a custom resource does not expose health in a way ArgoCD understands, define a Lua health customization based on its actual status conditions. Inspect the controller's status fields and establish what represents progressing, healthy, degraded, or suspended state. Test the health logic against real resource examples, including missing and contradictory conditions. Keep the customization version-controlled and review it when the custom-resource API changes. Incorrect health logic can cause premature deployment promotion or applications that never become healthy."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. How do you design ArgoCD disaster recovery?",
        "answer": "Document how to restore ArgoCD configuration, repository access, cluster registrations, projects, applications, secrets, and supporting storage. Keep declarative installation and configuration in version control, and protect credentials through a separate secure backup process. Ensure destination clusters remain recoverable independently of the ArgoCD control plane. Test rebuilding ArgoCD and reconnecting it to existing workloads without accidentally pruning resources. Validate application ownership, repository access, and reconciliation behavior during recovery exercises."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. How do you prevent accidental deletion of critical resources through ArgoCD pruning?",
        "answer": "Understand which resources ArgoCD owns and review deletion behavior before enabling prune. Use resource-specific deletion policies and Kubernetes finalizers where appropriate, especially for stateful resources and custom resources that trigger external cleanup. Require pull-request review for manifest removals and consider sync options that prevent unsafe deletion. Test pruning in a non-production environment and confirm that persistent data and external dependencies remain protected. Monitor deletion events and maintain tested backup and recovery procedures."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. How would you design an enterprise ArgoCD GitOps operating model?",
        "answer": "Use protected Git repositories as the source of truth, controlled ArgoCD projects, environment-specific destinations, and reusable ApplicationSet templates. Enforce pull-request reviews, policy checks, image provenance controls, and secure secret integration. Apply automated sync selectively, define health checks and progressive delivery, and monitor reconciliation failures and drift. Establish clear ownership for platform and application teams, audit changes, and test disaster recovery. Standardize promotion from development to production while preserving traceability to the exact Git revision."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "tool": "Helm",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "Q1. Explain Helm chart architecture and how Helm renders Kubernetes manifests.",
        "answer": "A Helm chart packages Kubernetes templates, default values, metadata, dependencies, and optional schema validation. During installation or upgrade, Helm combines chart defaults with supplied values and renders templates using its Go-template engine and built-in functions. The resulting manifests are submitted to the Kubernetes API server. A release stores revision and configuration information for lifecycle operations. Troubleshoot rendering by inspecting values, template conditions, chart dependencies, and the output of helm template before changing a production release."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. What is the difference between helm install, upgrade, rollback, and uninstall?",
        "answer": "helm install creates a new release from a chart. helm upgrade updates an existing release using a new chart or values, while helm rollback returns a release to a previous revision. helm uninstall removes the release-managed resources according to Helm behavior and configured resource policies. These operations have different data and availability implications. Inspect helm history and helm status before acting, and understand persistent-volume retention, hooks, and resources shared with other controllers before upgrading or uninstalling a production release."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. How do you troubleshoot a Helm chart that fails during template rendering?",
        "answer": "Run helm lint to identify chart structure and common template issues, then use helm template with the same values and release parameters as the failing deployment. Inspect YAML indentation, missing required values, incorrect types, template functions, and conditional blocks. Use --debug for additional rendering information. Compare the values file and chart version used locally with those used by CI/CD. Validate the rendered YAML against Kubernetes schemas or a test cluster before applying any correction to production."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. How do you manage Helm values across development, staging, and production?",
        "answer": "Keep common defaults in the chart's values.yaml and maintain separate environment-specific values files for differences such as replicas, resource limits, ingress hosts, and external service endpoints. Supply values in a predictable order because later files and command-line overrides take precedence. Avoid duplicating the entire values file across environments. Validate each environment's final rendered output and protect secrets outside ordinary values files. Record the exact chart version and values revision used for every production release."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. How do you upgrade a stateful application deployed through Helm without data loss?",
        "answer": "Review the chart release notes, Kubernetes API changes, StatefulSet configuration, storage classes, PVC behavior, and application-specific migration requirements. Back up persistent data and test the upgrade against a representative environment. Inspect the rendered diff and identify resource replacements, immutable fields, and database migrations. Use a controlled maintenance or rolling strategy, verify readiness and data integrity, and retain the previous chart version. A Helm rollback does not necessarily reverse database schema changes or restore deleted persistent data."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. What are Helm hooks and how can they cause production deployment problems?",
        "answer": "Helm hooks execute Kubernetes resources at lifecycle points such as pre-install, post-install, pre-upgrade, and post-upgrade. They are useful for migrations, validation, and cleanup, but can block an operation if they fail, time out, or remain in an unexpected state. Inspect hook annotations, Job status, pod logs, and deletion policies. Make hook jobs idempotent where possible and define realistic timeouts. Test both success and failure paths because hook-created resources may have lifecycle behavior different from ordinary release resources."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. How do you troubleshoot a Helm release stuck in pending-upgrade or pending-install?",
        "answer": "Inspect helm status and helm history to identify the current release revision and operation state. Check whether a previous deployment process is still running, whether hook jobs failed, and whether Kubernetes API operations timed out. Inspect release metadata and relevant cluster events before attempting recovery. Do not manually delete Helm release secrets without understanding the consequences. If recovery requires rollback or metadata repair, follow a tested procedure and verify that the resulting release state matches the actual Kubernetes resources."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. Explain Helm chart dependencies and how to manage them reliably.",
        "answer": "A chart can declare dependencies in Chart.yaml, with version constraints and repository information. Dependencies are resolved and packaged using Helm dependency commands, and the resulting chart archive or lock information should be controlled in the build process. Pin compatible versions and avoid floating dependency ranges for production releases. Inspect dependency metadata when templates unexpectedly change. Test the complete parent chart with the exact resolved dependencies and retain the package used for deployment so releases are reproducible."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. How do you design reusable Helm charts for multiple microservices?",
        "answer": "Create a consistent chart interface for common concerns such as deployments, services, ingress, probes, resources, service accounts, and autoscaling. Keep defaults safe and allow controlled customization through documented values. Avoid creating a chart with excessive conditionals that make behavior difficult to understand. Use helpers for repeated naming and labels, and validate input types with values.schema.json. Test several representative services and document which features are supported so teams do not rely on undocumented template behavior."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. How do you troubleshoot Helm upgrades that fail because of immutable Kubernetes fields?",
        "answer": "Render the proposed upgrade and compare it with the live resource to identify the immutable field that changed. Common examples include selected StatefulSet and Service fields. Determine whether the change can be corrected in values or requires a carefully planned resource replacement. Consider downtime, persistent data, selectors, and dependent resources before proceeding. Test the exact upgrade in a non-production cluster and use an approved migration plan rather than deleting production resources as a quick workaround."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. How do you secure Helm chart repositories and release artifacts?",
        "answer": "Use trusted chart repositories or an approved OCI registry, restrict publishing permissions, and pin chart versions. Verify provenance or signatures where supported and scan chart contents for embedded credentials, insecure defaults, and vulnerable images. Protect registry credentials in CI/CD and restrict who can modify release packages. Retain immutable release artifacts and their checksums so production deployments can be traced to reviewed content. Treat chart publication permissions as production deployment permissions because chart templates can create privileged resources."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. What is the purpose of values.schema.json in a Helm chart?",
        "answer": "values.schema.json validates chart values against a JSON Schema during Helm operations. It can enforce required properties, data types, allowed values, patterns, and nested object structure. This helps catch invalid configuration before Kubernetes resources are submitted. Define schemas for important production inputs such as image references, replica counts, resource requests, and service ports. Test valid and invalid values files and keep the schema synchronized with template behavior so it prevents mistakes without blocking legitimate environment configuration."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. How do you debug a Helm deployment where the chart succeeds but pods do not become Ready?",
        "answer": "A successful Helm operation does not guarantee application health. Inspect helm status, kubectl rollout status, pod descriptions, events, container logs, image pull errors, probes, PVC binding, and resource limits. Compare rendered configuration with the running workload and check external dependencies such as databases and DNS. Identify whether the issue is scheduling, startup, readiness, or application behavior. Correct the underlying cause and verify service endpoints and user-facing health before declaring the release successful."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. Explain Helm release revisions and how you use them during incident recovery.",
        "answer": "Each Helm upgrade creates a new release revision, allowing operators to inspect historical release state with helm history and retrieve previous manifests or values. During an incident, identify the last known healthy revision and compare it with the current revision before rollback. Check whether database migrations, external resources, or manual cluster changes occurred after the previous release. A rollback restores Helm-managed configuration but cannot guarantee reversal of external side effects. Validate application health and data compatibility after recovery."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. How do you manage secrets in Helm deployments?",
        "answer": "Avoid placing plaintext credentials in chart defaults, committed values files, or command-line arguments. Integrate with an approved external secrets manager or encrypted secret workflow, and restrict access to Helm release storage because release metadata may contain configuration values. Use Kubernetes RBAC and encryption at rest for Secret resources. Ensure CI logs do not print sensitive values and rotate credentials when exposed. Sensitive template output should be reviewed carefully because a rendered manifest can reveal secret data."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. How do you test Helm charts in a CI/CD pipeline?",
        "answer": "Run helm lint and render charts with representative environment values. Validate YAML syntax, schema constraints, Kubernetes API compatibility, and policy rules for security requirements. Deploy charts to disposable test clusters or namespaces and verify workload readiness and integration behavior. Test upgrades from previous chart versions as well as clean installations, because upgrade paths can reveal immutable-field and migration issues. Package and retain the exact tested chart artifact before promoting it to production."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. What is the difference between Helm template output and a live Kubernetes deployment?",
        "answer": "helm template renders manifests locally but does not submit them to the Kubernetes API server. It is useful for reviewing generated YAML, checking conditions, and debugging values. A live deployment also depends on API validation, admission webhooks, RBAC, scheduling, image availability, controllers, and runtime behavior. A manifest that renders correctly can still fail during installation or produce unhealthy pods. Compare rendered output with live objects and inspect Kubernetes events and controller status to diagnose deployment failures."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. How do you manage Helm chart API compatibility during Kubernetes upgrades?",
        "answer": "Inspect chart templates and dependencies for deprecated or removed Kubernetes API versions before upgrading the cluster. Render charts against the target Kubernetes version and use schema or API deprecation tools to identify incompatible resources. Review chart release notes and test the exact chart version in a cluster matching the planned target. Update templates and dependencies before the production cluster upgrade where possible. Verify CRDs and admission integrations separately because they can have upgrade requirements beyond ordinary Helm-managed resources."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. How do you troubleshoot unexpected changes caused by Helm value precedence?",
        "answer": "List every values source used by the deployment, including chart defaults, multiple -f files, --set arguments, and automation-generated overrides. Later values sources can override earlier ones, so compare the effective values with the intended configuration. Use helm get values and helm get values --all to inspect release values, and render the chart with the exact pipeline arguments. Correct the source of the override and make deployment parameters explicit. Avoid relying on undocumented local defaults that differ from CI."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. How would you establish an enterprise Helm release management strategy?",
        "answer": "Publish reviewed, versioned charts through a trusted repository or OCI registry. Standardize chart interfaces, values schemas, dependency pinning, security scanning, and CI validation. Promote immutable chart artifacts through development, staging, and production with environment-specific configuration and approval gates. Retain release history, package checksums, and deployment records. Test upgrades, rollbacks, and stateful migration paths. Define ownership for chart maintenance and ensure application teams can customize supported values without bypassing security and operational standards."
      }
