    "tool": "Prometheus",
    "category": "Advanced Interview Questions",
    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "Q1. How does Prometheus achieve high availability, and what limitations should engineers understand when running multiple Prometheus replicas?",
        "answer": "Prometheus high availability is commonly implemented by running two or more independent Prometheus servers with identical scrape configurations. Each replica collects the same targets and evaluates its own rules, so one server can continue serving queries and alerts when another fails. However, the replicas do not automatically share their local TSDB data or coordinate alert evaluations. External labels should identify the cluster and replica, and a compatible query layer such as Thanos or another deduplicating backend can merge replicated series. Engineers must also monitor scrape divergence, replica health, rule consistency, and downstream deduplication behavior."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. What is Prometheus metric cardinality, why can it become a production problem, and how do you control it?",
        "answer": "Cardinality is the number of distinct time series created by metric names and their label combinations. A metric containing labels such as user ID, request ID, session ID, or unrestricted URL paths can create millions of unique series. This increases memory consumption, ingestion cost, index size, query latency, and the risk of an out-of-memory failure. Engineers should inspect high-cardinality metrics, remove unbounded labels, normalize route names, and aggregate data before ingestion where appropriate. Track series growth, review instrumentation changes, and enforce metric-label standards during application development."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. Explain Prometheus federation and when you would use it instead of remote write or a long-term storage architecture.",
        "answer": "Federation allows one Prometheus server to scrape selected time series from another Prometheus server through its federation endpoint. It is useful when a global monitoring server needs aggregated metrics from multiple clusters or regions without collecting every raw application metric. The federated server should select only required metrics using appropriate match rules. Federation is not a replacement for remote write or durable long-term storage because it transfers selected samples through scraping and does not automatically provide complete historical retention. Use it for hierarchical monitoring and carefully design metric selection, scrape intervals, and network capacity."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. What happens in the Prometheus remote write pipeline, and how would you troubleshoot remote write failures?",
        "answer": "Prometheus remote write sends samples from its local storage pipeline to an external receiver using queued shards and HTTP requests. The WAL provides the source data for samples that must be transmitted, while queue configuration controls concurrent delivery and buffering. Failures can result from DNS problems, TLS errors, authentication failures, receiver throttling, network interruptions, or an overloaded destination. Inspect Prometheus logs, remote-write pending samples, failed requests, dropped samples, and receiver-side ingestion metrics. Correct the underlying problem before increasing queues, because excessive buffering can consume memory and delay recovery."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. How do Prometheus recording rules improve performance, and what risks arise from poorly designed recording rules?",
        "answer": "Recording rules periodically evaluate PromQL expressions and store their results as new time series. They are useful for expensive aggregations that are repeatedly requested by dashboards or alert rules, such as service-level request rates and error ratios. A good rule reduces repeated query work and provides predictable dashboard response times. Poorly designed rules can multiply cardinality, create duplicate or misleading metrics, and increase evaluation load. Validate expressions against representative data, use clear naming conventions, document label semantics, monitor rule evaluation duration, and avoid recording every possible aggregation combination."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. Explain the lifecycle of a Prometheus alert rule, including pending, firing, and resolved states.",
        "answer": "A Prometheus alert rule evaluates a PromQL expression at configured evaluation intervals. When the expression first returns a matching result, the alert enters the pending state if a for duration is configured. If the condition remains true for that duration, it becomes firing and can be sent to Alertmanager. When the expression stops returning the alerting series, the alert eventually becomes inactive or resolved according to the rule and notification flow. Engineers should choose the for duration based on expected transient behavior, use labels and annotations consistently, and test missing-data cases so short spikes do not create unnecessary pages."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. Describe the Prometheus relabeling pipeline and explain the difference between target relabeling and metric relabeling.",
        "answer": "Target relabeling is applied to discovered target labels before Prometheus decides which targets to scrape. It can rewrite addresses, add environment labels, keep selected targets, or drop unwanted targets. Metric relabeling is applied to individual samples after scraping and before ingestion, so it can remove or transform particular metrics and labels. Target relabeling is generally more efficient when a complete target should be excluded, while metric relabeling is useful for filtering selected samples. Incorrect regular expressions or action ordering can silently remove monitoring data, so validate relabel rules with representative discovery output and ingestion metrics."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. How would you investigate Kubernetes service discovery failures in Prometheus?",
        "answer": "Begin by checking the Prometheus target page and service-discovery page to determine whether Kubernetes objects are discovered and whether targets are dropped by relabeling. Verify the Kubernetes API endpoint, service account token, namespace permissions, Role or ClusterRole rules, and corresponding bindings. Confirm that the intended Services, Pods, EndpointSlices, and annotations or monitor resources match the discovery configuration. Review Prometheus logs for API authorization, timeout, and decoding errors. Finally, validate that network policies and pod ports allow scraping and that the discovered endpoint exposes the expected metrics path."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. A Prometheus target is DOWN even though the application is healthy. How would you isolate the cause?",
        "answer": "A healthy application does not guarantee that its metrics endpoint is reachable or correctly configured. Inspect the target error shown in the Prometheus UI, then test the exact scheme, host, port, path, and authentication requirements from the Prometheus network context. Check DNS resolution, routing, firewall rules, NetworkPolicies, TLS certificates, and response status codes. Verify that the endpoint returns valid Prometheus exposition data and does not exceed scrape timeout or response-size limits. Compare direct curl results with Prometheus logs and target labels before changing application health checks."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. Explain how Prometheus TSDB blocks, compaction, and retention work in a production environment.",
        "answer": "Prometheus stores samples in a time-series database using a write-ahead log and immutable block files. Recent samples are initially handled through the head block and WAL, while background compaction creates larger blocks for efficient querying and storage. Retention policies remove data based on configured time or size limits, so disk capacity must be planned for active data, compaction overhead, and temporary files. Monitor disk utilization, block health, compaction duration, and WAL growth. Do not manually delete active TSDB files while Prometheus is running, because doing so can corrupt the database or remove required recovery information."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. How would you respond to a Prometheus WAL corruption or unexpected startup failure?",
        "answer": "First preserve the data directory and capture Prometheus startup logs before attempting repairs. Determine whether the failure is caused by disk corruption, an interrupted filesystem operation, incompatible versions, insufficient disk space, or damaged WAL segments. Follow the documented recovery behavior for the deployed Prometheus version and test recovery on a copy of the data directory whenever possible. Do not immediately delete the entire TSDB directory, because that destroys historical data and may remove evidence. Restore from a valid backup or rebuild the server from a known configuration only after assessing the recovery options and operational impact."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. Compare Prometheus histograms and summaries, and explain the effect of each on distributed latency analysis.",
        "answer": "A histogram exposes bucket counters, count, and sum, allowing latency distributions to be aggregated across multiple application instances. Prometheus can calculate approximate quantiles from histogram buckets using histogram_quantile, provided bucket boundaries are suitable for the workload. A summary calculates quantiles inside the instrumented process, but its quantile values generally cannot be meaningfully aggregated across replicas. Histograms are therefore usually more appropriate for fleet-wide service latency dashboards and SLO analysis. Choose bucket boundaries around important latency objectives and keep an eye on the additional series created by each bucket and label combination."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. Explain the differences between rate(), irate(), and increase() in PromQL and give production guidance for selecting them.",
        "answer": "rate calculates the average per-second increase of a counter over a range, accounting for counter resets, and is generally suitable for dashboards and alerting. irate uses the last two samples in the range and highlights very short-term changes, but it can be noisy when traffic is irregular. increase estimates the total counter increase over the selected interval and is useful for reporting event counts. The range should contain enough samples for the scrape interval and evaluation needs. Applying these functions to gauges or using an excessively short range can produce misleading results, so confirm metric type and expected traffic behavior."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. What is vector matching in PromQL, and how do on(), ignoring(), group_left, and group_right affect query results?",
        "answer": "Vector matching determines how Prometheus combines two instant vectors in binary operations. By default, matching uses the common label set, while on specifies the exact labels that must match and ignoring removes selected labels from the matching criteria. group_left and group_right allow many-to-one or one-to-many relationships when the metric dimensions require them, and optional included labels can carry metadata from the many-side operation. Incorrect matching can drop series, multiply results, or produce duplicate-label errors. Inspect the labels on both operands and test cardinality assumptions before using grouping modifiers in production alert expressions."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. How should teams design metric names and labels to create a scalable Prometheus instrumentation standard?",
        "answer": "A scalable instrumentation standard uses metric names that describe the measured quantity and labels that represent bounded dimensions such as service, environment, region, and outcome. Counter names should communicate cumulative events, gauges should represent current values, and duration metrics should use consistent units such as seconds. Avoid user identifiers, request identifiers, raw URLs, and unbounded exception messages as labels. Document label meaning and allowed values, review instrumentation changes, and use recording rules for common aggregations. Consistent conventions make dashboards reusable and reduce accidental cardinality growth across teams."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. How does the Prometheus blackbox exporter work, and how would you use it to monitor an external service?",
        "answer": "The blackbox exporter performs probes such as HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, TCP, and ICMP checks on behalf of Prometheus. Prometheus scrapes the exporter with module and target parameters, and the exporter reports probe success, duration, and protocol-specific evidence. For an external HTTPS service, configure an HTTP probe that checks expected status codes, TLS behavior, and optionally response content. Separate probe execution from application instrumentation so failures can be detected even when the target application is unavailable. Monitor exporter availability, probe timeouts, DNS behavior, and network reachability from the actual monitoring environment."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. What security controls should be applied when deploying Prometheus exporters across production infrastructure?",
        "answer": "Exporters should expose only the metrics endpoint required by the monitoring system and should not be treated as general-purpose administrative interfaces. Restrict access using network policies, host firewalls, private network placement, and authentication or TLS where supported by the deployment. Avoid exposing process arguments, environment variables, sensitive filesystem paths, or credential-related metrics. Run exporters with least-privilege service accounts and keep versions patched. Validate that Prometheus can scrape the endpoint from approved networks while untrusted clients cannot access it, and include exporter configuration in security reviews."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. How would you implement multi-window, multi-burn-rate SLO alerts using Prometheus?",
        "answer": "A burn-rate alert compares the observed error-budget consumption rate with the allowed rate implied by the SLO. A multi-window approach checks both a short window and a longer window so that fast incidents can be detected without reacting to isolated noise. PromQL first calculates the error ratio using consistent request and error metrics, then compares it with burn-rate thresholds derived from the SLO objective and alerting policy. Use separate labels and annotations to identify the service and affected objective. Validate traffic volume, missing series, counter resets, and the relationship between alert windows before routing pages to production responders."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. Prometheus queries are slow after the number of monitored services increased. How would you diagnose and reduce query load?",
        "answer": "Start by correlating query latency with active series count, ingestion rate, memory use, CPU, disk I/O, and the Prometheus query log where enabled. Identify expensive dashboard expressions, broad range selectors, high-cardinality label combinations, and repeated aggregations over long time windows. Use recording rules for frequently reused expensive expressions, narrow label matchers, and reduce unnecessary scrape volume or retention where appropriate. Check dashboard refresh frequency and concurrent users as well. Any optimization must preserve the metrics needed for incident response and SLO calculations, so compare query results before and after the change."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. When should an organization move from standalone Prometheus to a distributed or long-term metrics architecture?",
        "answer": "A standalone Prometheus server may become insufficient when retention requirements, regional scale, tenant isolation, query concurrency, or availability expectations exceed what one local TSDB can safely provide. A long-term architecture commonly combines local Prometheus scraping with remote write, object storage, and a scalable query layer such as Thanos, Mimir, or Cortex. Evaluate ingestion rate, series count, retention duration, query patterns, recovery objectives, and operational skills before selecting a platform. Plan for deduplication, tenant boundaries, compaction, storage costs, and failure behavior rather than assuming that remote storage automatically removes every operational limit."
      }
    ]
  },
