    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "Q1. How would you design Grafana high availability for a production monitoring platform?",
        "answer": "A highly available Grafana deployment runs multiple application replicas behind a load balancer and uses a shared external database such as PostgreSQL or MySQL rather than relying on local SQLite storage. All replicas must use consistent configuration, encryption secrets, authentication settings, and compatible plugin versions. Session handling and any required shared state must be configured according to the chosen authentication and deployment model. Use persistent or shared storage for required files, health checks for routing, and tested backups for the database and configuration. Monitor replica health, database availability, request latency, and alert delivery."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. Explain Grafana provisioning and how you would manage dashboards and data sources through GitOps.",
        "answer": "Grafana provisioning allows data sources, dashboards, alert rules, and other supported resources to be defined declaratively in files. A GitOps workflow stores these definitions in version control, validates them in pull requests, and deploys approved changes through CI/CD or configuration management. This reduces manual drift and makes environments repeatable. Teams should define ownership, folder placement, stable identifiers, and environment-specific variables carefully. Provisioning behavior can overwrite or conflict with UI-managed changes, so establish whether resources are code-owned or manually managed and test the deployment process in a non-production Grafana instance."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. What are the recommended security practices for managing Grafana data source credentials?",
        "answer": "Data source credentials should be stored using Grafana secure settings or an approved external secret-management integration rather than embedded in dashboard JSON or source control. Restrict who can create and edit data sources, use least-privilege service credentials, and rotate secrets according to the organization policy. Avoid exposing credentials through provisioning files, debug logs, screenshots, or browser-visible variables. Use TLS for connections to Grafana and data sources, and verify that backups and deployment artifacts protect encrypted secret material. Test credential rotation and access revocation before relying on them during an incident."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. How would you troubleshoot a Grafana dashboard that loads slowly or times out?",
        "answer": "Inspect browser developer tools and Grafana server logs to distinguish slow dashboard rendering from slow data-source queries or network failures. Review dashboard refresh intervals, panel count, time range, repeated queries, transformations, and template-variable dependencies. Test the underlying query directly in Explore and inspect data-source response time and timeout limits. Reduce unnecessary panels, use recording rules or pre-aggregated data for expensive metrics, and avoid loading excessive historical ranges by default. Also monitor Grafana CPU, memory, database latency, and concurrent users because the application itself can become the bottleneck."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. How do Grafana dashboard variables work, and what risks arise from poorly designed variable queries?",
        "answer": "Dashboard variables dynamically provide values that can be reused in panel queries, titles, links, and other dashboard settings. Variables can depend on data-source queries or on other variables, enabling environment, cluster, namespace, and service selection. Poorly designed variables can issue expensive queries whenever a dashboard loads, create cascading refresh delays, or return unbounded values that make panels slow. Use focused label filters, chained variables where appropriate, sensible defaults, and an All option that does not expand into a huge query. Validate variable behavior when data is empty or when users select multiple values."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. When should Grafana transformations be used, and why should they not replace all data-source query logic?",
        "answer": "Grafana transformations manipulate returned data frames to join, filter, rename, calculate, or organize fields for visualization. They are useful when a panel needs presentation-level changes or when combining limited result sets that are not practical to express in one query. However, transformations happen after data has been retrieved, so they do not necessarily reduce backend query cost or network transfer. Complex calculations are often better implemented in PromQL, SQL, or the source system where they can use indexes and aggregation efficiently. Keep transformation chains understandable and test their behavior when query results are missing or change shape."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. Explain Grafana unified alerting architecture and how alert evaluation differs from dashboard visualization.",
        "answer": "Unified alerting evaluates alert rules independently of whether a dashboard is open in a browser. Rules query their configured data sources on a schedule, apply conditions and expressions, and move through states such as Normal, Pending, Alerting, and Recovering according to the rule configuration. Notification policies then route alert instances to contact points using labels and grouping rules. This separation means a panel can render successfully while an alert rule fails due to evaluation permissions, query differences, or missing data. Test alert rules directly, inspect evaluation history, and verify notification delivery rather than relying only on dashboard appearance."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. How do Grafana notification policies and contact points work together in a multi-team environment?",
        "answer": "Contact points define where notifications are delivered, such as email, chat platforms, incident-management systems, or webhooks. Notification policies determine which contact point receives an alert based on labels and can apply grouping, timing, and child-policy routing. In a multi-team environment, use consistent labels such as team, service, environment, and severity so ownership is predictable. Configure grouping and repeat intervals to reduce notification storms while preserving critical incident visibility. Test delivery credentials, escalation behavior, grouping keys, and fallback routes, and document who owns each policy."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. How should engineers investigate Grafana alerts that show No Data or Error instead of a normal alert state?",
        "answer": "No Data means the alert evaluation did not receive the expected series or values, while Error indicates that the evaluation encountered a problem such as a failed query, timeout, permission issue, or invalid expression. Inspect the alert rule query, evaluation history, data-source health, query inspector output, and Grafana logs. Confirm whether the metric disappeared because the workload stopped reporting, labels changed, or the time range is incorrect. Configure No Data and Error handling deliberately according to business risk, because treating missing telemetry as healthy can hide outages while paging on harmless gaps creates noise."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. What controls should be used to manage Grafana plugins safely in production?",
        "answer": "Plugins extend Grafana functionality but introduce additional code, compatibility, and supply-chain risk. Install plugins only from trusted sources, review plugin permissions and maintenance status, pin approved versions, and test upgrades in a staging environment. Prefer signed plugins and enforce the organization policy for unsigned plugin installation rather than disabling signature checks broadly. Monitor plugin-related startup errors and resource use, and remove plugins that are no longer required. Include plugin versions in deployment configuration so replicas remain consistent and rollback can restore a known-good plugin set."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. How would you optimize a Grafana dashboard that contains hundreds of panels and many concurrent viewers?",
        "answer": "Reduce the number of simultaneously rendered panels and avoid repeating expensive queries across many visualizations. Set reasonable default time ranges and refresh intervals, use dashboard variables to narrow the selected environment or service, and use recording rules or pre-aggregated datasets for commonly requested calculations. Inspect query inspector timings to identify slow panels and review the data-source query limits. Consider splitting one oversized dashboard into focused operational views and use library panels when common visualizations must be reused. Validate the improvements under realistic concurrent load instead of judging performance from a single browser session."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. How do you design Grafana dashboards for Loki logs without creating excessive label cardinality?",
        "answer": "For Loki, labels should represent a small set of stable dimensions such as cluster, namespace, application, and environment. High-cardinality values such as request IDs, user IDs, full URLs, and timestamps should remain in the log body or structured metadata rather than becoming indexed labels. Grafana dashboards should use selective LogQL label matchers before applying content filters so queries scan less data. Provide useful variables for service and namespace selection, and use consistent log fields for correlation. Monitor query performance and ingestion behavior because an unsafe label strategy can increase storage cost and make log queries difficult to operate."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. A Grafana panel reports a data-source timeout. How would you identify whether the issue is in Grafana or the backend?",
        "answer": "Use the panel query inspector to capture the actual request, query parameters, execution duration, and returned error. Test the same query directly against the backend using an authorized method from the Grafana network location. If the backend is slow independently, inspect its workload, query plan, storage latency, and resource saturation. If direct requests succeed but Grafana times out, review Grafana proxy settings, data-source timeout configuration, request limits, and network intermediaries. Increase timeouts only after confirming that the query is legitimately expensive and that longer requests will not overload the service."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. Explain Grafana RBAC and how you would separate access for platform, application, and business teams.",
        "answer": "Grafana role-based access control assigns permissions to users, teams, service accounts, folders, and supported resources. A platform team may manage data sources and shared configuration, while application teams receive edit rights only for their owned folders and dashboards. Business users can receive view-only access to approved reports. Use team synchronization with the identity provider where possible, avoid broad administrator grants, and review service-account permissions separately from human access. Test access with representative users and verify that folder permissions, data-source permissions, and organization boundaries enforce the intended separation."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. How would you integrate Grafana with enterprise authentication using OAuth or an authentication proxy?",
        "answer": "Enterprise authentication can be integrated through supported OAuth providers or an authentication proxy that validates identity before forwarding requests to Grafana. Configure trusted issuer or proxy settings, client credentials, redirect URLs, signing certificates, and group or role mapping carefully. The proxy must prevent direct untrusted access to Grafana when identity headers are used, because spoofed headers could grant unauthorized access. Test login, logout, token expiration, group changes, and administrator recovery. Keep a documented break-glass procedure that does not weaken the normal authentication controls."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. What is the purpose of Grafana annotations, and how can they improve incident investigation?",
        "answer": "Annotations add time-based context to dashboards, such as deployment events, configuration changes, maintenance windows, and incident start or recovery markers. They help responders correlate metric changes with operational events instead of investigating charts without release context. Annotations can be created manually, through supported APIs, or from integrated systems. Use consistent tags and include a concise event description with a reference to the authoritative deployment or incident record. Restrict write access appropriately and avoid treating an annotation as proof of causation; it is investigative evidence that must be correlated with logs, traces, and metrics."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. How would you manage dashboard versioning and prevent configuration drift across development, staging, and production?",
        "answer": "Store dashboard definitions in version control and promote reviewed changes through a controlled deployment pipeline. Use stable dashboard UIDs, environment-aware data-source references, and provisioning or API automation to deploy the same logical dashboard across environments. Avoid editing provisioned dashboards manually in production unless the workflow explicitly exports and commits those changes. Compare deployed definitions during release validation and retain previous versions for rollback. Assign clear ownership and review dashboard changes like application code so broken queries, accidental data exposure, or destructive edits are caught before production rollout."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. How can Grafana multi-organization or tenant separation be used, and what should be validated before relying on it?",
        "answer": "Grafana organizations provide logical separation for supported dashboards, users, folders, and data-source configuration within an instance. They can be useful for separating teams or customer views, but the exact isolation boundary depends on the configured authentication, permissions, data-source behavior, and deployment architecture. Validate that users cannot access another organization through shared credentials, APIs, service accounts, or incorrectly assigned roles. For strong customer isolation requirements, evaluate dedicated instances or an architecture with explicit tenant-aware data access. Document the isolation model and test it with negative authorization tests."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. What are the main considerations when enabling Grafana image rendering or scheduled reporting at scale?",
        "answer": "Image rendering and reporting may require a rendering service or browser-based worker, and each render consumes CPU, memory, browser processes, and data-source query capacity. Large dashboards, high-resolution images, and overlapping schedules can create resource spikes or report delays. Restrict who can request reports, use appropriate timeouts and concurrency limits, and monitor rendering workers independently from Grafana web replicas. Validate fonts, panel plugins, authentication, and network access in the rendering environment. Treat report delivery as a separate operational workflow with failure alerts and controlled access to generated files."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. Describe a safe Grafana upgrade and rollback strategy for a production monitoring platform.",
        "answer": "A safe upgrade starts with reviewing release notes, plugin compatibility, database migration requirements, and supported upgrade paths. Back up the Grafana database, configuration, provisioning files, dashboards where necessary, and secret-management references before changing versions. Test the exact target version against a representative staging environment and validate login, data sources, dashboards, alert evaluation, notification delivery, and plugins. Upgrade replicas using a controlled rollout with health checks and monitor logs and latency. Database migrations may not be reversible by simply downgrading the binary, so rollback must follow a tested restore or vendor-supported recovery plan."
      }
    ]
