    "questions": [
      {
        "question": "Q1. Explain the architecture of Sonatype Nexus Repository Manager and how its major components work together.",
        "answer": "Nexus Repository Manager provides centralized storage and distribution for software components, packages, and container images. Its architecture includes repository definitions, blob stores, metadata databases, security and authorization services, scheduled tasks, and client-facing APIs. Developers and CI pipelines publish artifacts to hosted repositories, retrieve approved dependencies through proxy repositories, and use repository groups to access multiple repositories through one endpoint. The database stores repository configuration and metadata, while blob stores retain binary content. In production, administrators must monitor both metadata storage and blob capacity, protect the database and blob stores together during backups, and maintain repository access policies."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q2. Compare hosted, proxy, and group repositories in Nexus and explain when each should be used in an enterprise environment.",
        "answer": "A hosted repository stores artifacts that an organization publishes and controls, such as internal Maven releases, snapshots, npm packages, or Docker images. A proxy repository caches components from a remote source such as Maven Central or an approved public registry, reducing repeated external downloads and allowing centralized policy enforcement. A group repository combines multiple hosted and proxy repositories behind one client endpoint. For example, a Maven group can expose internal releases, internal snapshots, and Maven Central through a single URL. Repository order, version policies, content selectors, and deployment permissions should be designed carefully so clients resolve the intended artifact source."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q3. Explain Nexus blob stores and how you would plan storage architecture for a large production repository.",
        "answer": "A blob store is the storage layer where Nexus keeps the binary content of repository components. Depending on the deployment and supported configuration, blob stores can use local filesystem storage or supported object-storage backends. Repository metadata and binary content are related but are not interchangeable, so administrators must understand how the configured database references stored blobs. For a large production installation, estimate growth from artifact retention, Docker layers, build frequency, and cleanup policies. Monitor capacity, latency, IOPS, and backend availability. Separate storage planning from metadata database sizing, and test backup and restore procedures for the complete repository state."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q4. How would you design Nexus repository cleanup policies without accidentally deleting artifacts required by production deployments?",
        "answer": "A safe cleanup strategy begins by identifying repository formats, release and snapshot policies, artifact age, download history, and retention obligations. Configure cleanup policies to select only artifacts that meet approved criteria, then associate them with the appropriate repositories through cleanup tasks. Do not assume that an old artifact is unused: production rollback, compliance, and reproducible builds may depend on historical versions. Start with a report or controlled test repository where possible, review candidate deletions, and obtain approval for production policies. Preserve release versions according to retention requirements, monitor task results, and maintain recoverable backups before enabling destructive cleanup."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q5. Describe a high-availability architecture for Nexus and the production considerations involved.",
        "answer": "A highly available Nexus design must account for the application tier, database, blob storage, network access, and load balancing. The supported topology depends on the Nexus edition, version, and deployment model, so administrators must follow the vendor's documented clustering and external database requirements rather than simply starting multiple application nodes against arbitrary shared storage. The load balancer should use meaningful health checks and controlled connection behavior. Database and blob storage must meet availability and performance requirements. Test node failure, database interruption, storage failure, and recovery procedures, and document which failures are automatically handled versus requiring operator intervention."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q6. How do you secure Nexus repositories against unauthorized artifact uploads, downloads, and dependency substitution?",
        "answer": "Secure Nexus by integrating identity management where appropriate, assigning least-privilege roles, separating browse, read, and deployment permissions, and restricting anonymous access to repositories that genuinely require it. Use TLS for client and administrative traffic, protect service credentials in a secrets manager, and rotate credentials according to policy. Restrict who can modify repository configuration, routing rules, and content selectors. Use approved proxy sources and prevent untrusted repositories from becoming preferred dependency sources. Enable audit logging and monitor unusual uploads, permission changes, and unexpected artifact versions. CI pipelines should use dedicated service identities rather than shared administrator credentials."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q7. Explain how Nexus repository groups affect Maven dependency resolution and how you would troubleshoot an incorrect artifact version being downloaded.",
        "answer": "A Maven group presents multiple repositories through one URL, allowing a build to request dependencies without knowing the physical repository that contains each component. Resolution can be affected by repository membership, ordering, metadata, cached proxy responses, and the coordinates requested by the POM. To investigate an incorrect version, capture the exact group URL and coordinates, inspect the effective POM and dependency tree, and check which member repository supplied the artifact. Review proxy cache state and Maven local cache separately. Correct the repository configuration or dependency declaration, then validate from a clean or controlled client cache without deleting shared repository content blindly."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q8. How would you configure Nexus for Docker image storage and secure image distribution to Kubernetes or EKS?",
        "answer": "A Nexus Docker repository can host private images, proxy an approved remote registry, or expose a group endpoint depending on the deployment design and supported configuration. Configure the connector or ingress endpoint, TLS, repository permissions, and Docker client authentication correctly. CI should build an image, tag it with an immutable release identifier, scan it, and push it using a dedicated deployment identity. Kubernetes nodes or workloads must have an approved method to authenticate when pulling private images. Validate DNS, certificates, registry endpoints, image pull secrets or node-level credentials, and repository permissions. Avoid relying only on mutable tags such as latest for production releases."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q9. Explain Nexus content selectors and how they can provide fine-grained repository access control.",
        "answer": "Content selectors allow administrators to define which repository content a role can access based on supported component attributes and patterns. They are useful when multiple teams share a repository but must only read or manage their own namespaces, group IDs, package scopes, or paths. A selector must be paired with the appropriate repository privilege and role assignment; creating a selector alone does not grant or deny access. Test the policy with representative user accounts and both permitted and prohibited coordinates. Review edge cases such as wildcard matching, case sensitivity, and repository format behavior, and keep selector ownership documented so future changes do not unintentionally broaden access."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q10. How would you investigate and resolve a Nexus disk-space incident affecting artifact uploads and downloads?",
        "answer": "First establish whether the exhausted resource is the blob-store filesystem, database volume, temporary directory, container filesystem, or an external storage backend. Check filesystem capacity and inode usage with commands such as df -h and df -i, then correlate the affected mount with Nexus configuration and application logs. Review repository growth, Docker layer accumulation, temporary files, backup retention, and cleanup task history. Do not manually delete blob files because metadata may still reference them. Restore service capacity through approved storage expansion or validated cleanup policies, then test uploads and downloads, monitor storage growth, and add capacity alerts with sufficient operational headroom."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q11. Explain the relationship between Nexus database backups and blob-store backups, and describe a reliable recovery approach.",
        "answer": "A recoverable Nexus backup must preserve the database metadata and the binary content stored in the configured blob stores in a mutually consistent state. Backing up only the database can leave metadata pointing to missing binaries, while backing up only blob storage can leave components undiscoverable because repository metadata is absent. Follow the backup method supported for the installed Nexus version and deployment topology, including the required quiescence or consistency procedure. Protect backup copies, test restoration in an isolated environment, and verify representative component downloads after recovery. Document recovery point and recovery time objectives and regularly rehearse the complete process."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q12. How do Nexus staging repositories and release promotion support controlled software delivery?",
        "answer": "Staging workflows allow teams to publish candidate artifacts into a controlled repository area, run validation, and promote approved content into a release repository according to organizational policy and available Nexus capabilities. This separates build publication from release approval and reduces the risk of exposing incomplete artifacts to consumers. A release process should verify artifact coordinates, checksums, signatures where required, test evidence, and approval records before promotion. Avoid rebuilding the application during promotion because a rebuild can produce different bytes. Record the promoted artifact identity and ensure downstream deployment pipelines consume the approved immutable version."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q13. Describe how Nexus proxy caching works and how you would troubleshoot stale or unavailable remote dependencies.",
        "answer": "A proxy repository retrieves components from a configured remote source and caches content and metadata according to repository behavior and cache settings. This reduces repeated external traffic and provides a centralized dependency endpoint, but cached metadata or negative lookup results can affect what clients see. Start by identifying the requested coordinates, remote URL, repository status, and Nexus request logs. Test remote reachability, DNS, TLS trust, proxy configuration, and authentication. Inspect cache and metadata settings before invalidating anything. Correct the upstream or network issue, use supported cache invalidation when justified, and validate resolution through the Nexus endpoint from a clean test client."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q14. How should administrators plan Nexus upgrades in a production environment with critical CI/CD dependencies?",
        "answer": "A production upgrade should begin with checking the supported upgrade path, release notes, compatibility requirements, database and Java requirements, storage considerations, and any required intermediate versions. Inventory repository formats, plugins, integrations, authentication settings, and automation clients. Create verified backups of the database and blob stores using the documented procedure, then rehearse the upgrade on a representative non-production copy. Schedule a maintenance window, stop or drain clients as required, apply the upgrade, and inspect startup and migration logs. Validate authentication, repository browsing, artifact upload and download, Docker pulls, and CI builds before reopening normal traffic."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q15. Explain how Nexus REST APIs can be used for repository administration and what controls are required for automation.",
        "answer": "Nexus exposes REST endpoints that can support administrative automation such as repository inspection, component queries, task operations, and other capabilities available in the installed version. Automation should use documented API endpoints and a dedicated service account with only the permissions required for its task. Store credentials securely, use TLS, handle HTTP status codes and pagination, and implement timeouts and retry logic for transient failures. Avoid embedding administrator passwords in scripts or source control. Test automation against a non-production instance, log actions and response identifiers, and validate the final repository state rather than treating an HTTP success response as proof that every intended artifact operation completed."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q16. How would you troubleshoot Nexus authentication failures when CI pipelines suddenly receive HTTP 401 or 403 responses?",
        "answer": "HTTP 401 commonly indicates that the request is unauthenticated or the supplied credentials are not accepted, while HTTP 403 indicates that the authenticated identity is not authorized for the requested operation, although exact behavior can depend on the endpoint and configuration. Check the pipeline secret reference, credential rotation history, username, token validity, and whether the client is sending credentials to the intended repository endpoint. Then inspect Nexus user, role, privilege, and content-selector assignments. Review reverse-proxy authentication behavior and server logs. Test read and deploy permissions separately with a dedicated account, update only the required permission, and verify the pipeline without exposing credentials in logs."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q17. Explain how you would monitor Nexus health and establish actionable production alerts.",
        "answer": "Nexus monitoring should cover application availability, request latency and error rates, JVM heap and garbage collection, CPU, memory, database health, blob-store capacity, filesystem inodes, and scheduled task failures. Use the monitoring and metrics interfaces supported by the deployed version, and combine them with host, container, database, and load-balancer telemetry. Alerts should identify symptoms that affect users, such as failed artifact downloads, upload errors, sustained high latency, or storage nearing capacity. Avoid alerting only on process uptime because a running process may still be unable to serve repositories. Define ownership, escalation thresholds, dashboards, and runbooks for each critical signal."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q18. What is the role of repository routing rules in Nexus, and how can they prevent dependency confusion?",
        "answer": "Repository routing rules can constrain which paths or requests are allowed through particular proxy repositories, depending on the repository format and Nexus capabilities. They help organizations limit access to approved namespaces or prevent a proxy from serving components that should only originate from internal hosted repositories. A secure design also requires controlled repository group membership, trusted upstream sources, namespace ownership, and restricted publishing permissions. Review routing behavior using representative coordinates and confirm that internal package names cannot be unintentionally resolved from an untrusted public source. Document exceptions and monitor dependency changes so a policy does not silently weaken over time."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q19. How would you diagnose slow artifact downloads from Nexus when the application process appears healthy?",
        "answer": "Investigate the full request path instead of assuming the Nexus JVM is the only bottleneck. Measure client-to-load-balancer latency, reverse-proxy behavior, Nexus request timing, database response time, blob-store read latency, and network throughput. Compare small and large artifacts, hosted and proxied repositories, and requests from different network locations. Inspect CPU, garbage collection, thread usage, connection pools, storage IOPS, and concurrent request volume. Check whether a remote proxy is waiting on an upstream server. Apply changes only after identifying the limiting layer, then repeat controlled download tests and compare latency percentiles and error rates."
      },
      {
        "question": "Q20. Design an enterprise artifact governance strategy using Nexus across development, testing, and production environments.",
        "answer": "An enterprise governance strategy should define approved repositories, naming and versioning standards, immutable release rules, snapshot retention, dependency source controls, access ownership, and promotion approvals. Developers and CI should consume dependencies through approved proxy or group endpoints, while publishing to hosted repositories using dedicated identities. Integrate vulnerability scanning, license review, checksums or signatures, and software bill-of-materials evidence into the release process. Separate development snapshots from production releases and promote the exact validated artifact rather than rebuilding it. Monitor repository health, audit administrative changes, review exceptions, and test backup, restore, and disaster-recovery procedures."
      }
    ]
